WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:12.275
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.275 --> 00:00:13.440
HARRIS: Good evening.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:18.930
My name is Ellen
Harris, and on behalf

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:20.700
of the Office of
the Arts at MIT,

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.280
I am happy to welcome
you to the Institute

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:26.220
and to our conference, "The
Public Patron," drafting

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:30.360
a mandate for a
Federal Arts Agency.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:31.740
The conference is
being presented

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:33.480
by the Office of
the Arts and was

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:36.270
organized by Mark Palmgren,
Director of the Council

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:38.920
for the Arts at MIT.

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:41.410
Before tonight's
program actually begins,

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:44.380
I would like to take this
opportunity to outline briefly

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:46.940
the schedule of events.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:49.690
First, we hope all
of you here tonight

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:51.850
are planning to attend
tomorrow's panel

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:55.930
discussions, which will also be
held here at Kresge Auditorium,

00:00:55.930 --> 00:00:59.050
beginning at 9:15 AM.

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:02.230
In the, morning the panel will
present and discuss issues

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:06.450
relating to the funding
of creative expression.

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:08.130
And then after a
break for lunch,

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:11.700
a second panel will discuss the
role of a Federal Arts Funding

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:17.630
Agency in terms of education
in the arts beginning at 1:30.

00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:19.400
Our panel is for
both discussions,

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:23.750
comprising an outstanding roster
of artists, educators, critics,

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:27.080
and arts administrators from
across the country, all of whom

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:31.080
have faced these issues
in their own work.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:34.440
At 4:15, following the
panels, Representative Louise

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:37.140
Slaughter, Chairman of the
Congressional Arts Caucus,

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:38.820
will present the
concluding keynote

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.490
address, after which
she will take questions

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:44.210
from the audience.

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:46.320
Registration for
the day's events,

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:52.730
including lunch, is $35,
$25 for students with ID.

00:01:52.730 --> 00:01:54.380
If you haven't
already registered,

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:57.790
I invite you to do so following
this evening's program

00:01:57.790 --> 00:02:02.770
or tomorrow morning here at
Kresge, starting at 8:00 AM.

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:05.740
This evening, Charles M.
Vest, President of MIT,

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:10.030
will welcome you, after which
Senator Edward M. Kennedy

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:13.210
and author and art critic Robert
Hughes will present the opening

00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:15.580
keynote addresses.

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:18.310
Mr. Hughes has kindly
agreed to accept questions

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:20.170
after his remarks.

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:23.560
If you wish to ask a
question, please step up

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:27.580
to one of the floor microphones
in the two central aisles.

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:31.630
And when you are recognized,
please identify yourself.

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:35.140
I am sure that it will be
appreciated by all of us

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:38.050
if this time is used
for questions only,

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:40.450
and if you will keep
your questions brief

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:43.630
and to the point.

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:46.720
I am pleased to announce that
the Office of the Arts at MIT

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:49.570
will be publishing the
proceedings of the conference.

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:52.060
The proceedings will be
distributed to all conference

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:55.030
registrants, key government
officials, members

00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:58.240
and staff of the congressional
committees involved in the NEA

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:01.630
reauthorization and
reappropriation hearings,

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:04.810
and arts organizations
across the country.

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:07.300
MIT will also work
towards developing

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:10.900
an interdisciplinary policy
paper to consider models

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:14.290
for maintaining ongoing
federal support for the arts

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:18.040
and humanities, incorporating
themes and insights

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:20.773
from our deliberations
here this weekend.

00:03:20.773 --> 00:03:24.840
And now I am pleased to
introduce to you Charles M.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:29.642
Vest, President of MIT

00:03:29.642 --> 00:03:32.127
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:44.316 --> 00:03:45.690
VEST: Thank you
very much, Ellen.

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:47.700
It's a great pleasure
to welcome all of you

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:50.700
to MIT and to this national
conference on the roles

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:53.670
and responsibilities
of a federal art agency

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:56.330
in the United States today.

00:03:56.330 --> 00:04:00.800
Some may see MIT, focused as it
is on science and engineering,

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:05.050
as a rather odd choice of
venue for such a conference.

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:07.470
But as most of you
already know, the arts

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:10.830
are indeed a vital aspect of
life here at the Institute.

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:13.740
You need only look around
at our outdoor sculptures,

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:15.570
at our galleries,
and when school

00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:19.170
is in session, at the multitude
of courses and programs

00:04:19.170 --> 00:04:22.410
and music, theater writing,
photography, dance,

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:25.620
and film that engage our
faculty, our students,

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:27.490
and our staff.

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:29.320
One of the great
things about MIT

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:31.930
is that this is
a hands-on place.

00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:35.840
We make things here, and much of
our learning is through doing.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:38.350
I believe that that is one
of the common denominators

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:41.845
of artistic, technological,
and scientific activity,

00:04:41.845 --> 00:04:44.500
and that is one of the reasons
that the arts are indeed

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:48.490
such a vital part
of this institution.

00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:50.710
Much of what we
do in these fields

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:54.520
is overseen and coordinated by
our Office for the Arts, which

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:56.740
is sponsoring this conference.

00:04:56.740 --> 00:04:59.770
And a great deal of the support
we receive for these programs

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:02.740
comes for the Council
of the Arts at MIT,

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:06.190
the volunteer advocacy
group of alumni, alumnae,

00:05:06.190 --> 00:05:09.550
and friends that was
established over 20 years ago

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:12.310
to foster the creative
and performing arts

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:14.020
here at the Institute.

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:17.440
So clearly, the arts are an
integral part of what we are

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:19.280
and what we do.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:22.690
But why is it important for
scientists and engineers

00:05:22.690 --> 00:05:26.140
to study and participate
in the creative arts?

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:30.700
Quite simply, involvement in the
arts broadens one's perspective

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:33.820
and opens the mind
to new possibilities.

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:35.770
The arts spring
from and concentrate

00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:38.810
on the human condition,
our history, values,

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:42.060
and cultures, the ways
we find inspiration,

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:44.650
the ways in which we
identify and communicate

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:46.780
ideas and emotions.

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:49.810
And they help us to see
the world in new ways.

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:51.970
It's like looking at
an image upside down

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:54.550
to see it for what it
is and not for what

00:05:54.550 --> 00:05:57.160
our eyes are trained to see.

00:05:57.160 --> 00:06:00.310
When we develop our ability
to think creatively,

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:02.200
we are able to
find new solutions

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:04.480
to the problems on
which we are working,

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:08.110
both in our laboratories
and in our lives.

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:10.960
Creative activity is
creative activity,

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:13.300
whether it is the
establishment of a new theorem

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:17.320
in abstract mathematics or
the non-obvious optimization

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:19.810
of a complex
engineering process,

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:23.030
or the expression of
an artistic insight.

00:06:23.030 --> 00:06:25.790
Education in the arts,
as in the sciences,

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:29.150
helps us to develop and
flex our creative muscle.

00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:31.070
It is rigorous exercise.

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:33.120
It is good for us.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:35.310
One of our professors
who wonderfully embodies

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:38.000
both of these worlds
is Alan Lightman,

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:40.410
a physicist who heads
our program in writing

00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:42.600
and humanistic studies.

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:45.960
He's the author of the
widely acclaimed novel

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:47.730
Einstein's Dreams.

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:51.330
And in a recent interview,
Alan explained this duality

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:54.140
of his interests this way.

00:06:54.140 --> 00:06:57.710
Human beings have this
very rational, logical need

00:06:57.710 --> 00:06:59.900
to order our perception.

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:02.120
But we also need to
take away the fetters

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:06.140
and fly free, escape
from the physical world.

00:07:06.140 --> 00:07:08.450
We are both scientists
and artists.

00:07:08.450 --> 00:07:10.280
It is a wonderful tension.

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:11.810
We are whole people.

00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:16.510
We can't cut off one side
of ourselves from the other.

00:07:16.510 --> 00:07:19.490
And I cannot think of a better
explanation for why the arts

00:07:19.490 --> 00:07:22.580
are essential to a
complete education, even,

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:24.890
or perhaps I should
say especially,

00:07:24.890 --> 00:07:27.080
in a place like MIT.

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:30.650
Science and mathematical
literacy cannot be developed

00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:33.360
in the absence of
general literacy.

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:36.980
As Professor Ellen Harris,
MIT'S Associate Provost

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:39.275
for the Arts, who you
just met, has noted,

00:07:39.275 --> 00:07:41.150
"The arts offer--"
and I quote her--

00:07:41.150 --> 00:07:44.270
"a gateway to improving
learning overall

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:48.020
by enlarging the imagination,
by instilling both discipline

00:07:48.020 --> 00:07:51.080
and wonder, and by teaching
flexible and creative

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:54.140
approaches to problem solving."

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:56.510
In short, the
sciences and the arts

00:07:56.510 --> 00:08:01.900
do and can inform and
reinforce each other.

00:08:01.900 --> 00:08:04.840
So much for the role of
the arts in education

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:07.540
in the place of the
arts at this Institute.

00:08:07.540 --> 00:08:09.520
What about the role of
the federal government

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:12.400
as a patron of the arts?

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:14.110
What are the principles
to be followed

00:08:14.110 --> 00:08:15.820
and the goals to be achieved?

00:08:15.820 --> 00:08:18.850
That is what you are here
to discuss and to debate,

00:08:18.850 --> 00:08:22.450
and if the gods are with
us, perhaps even resolve.

00:08:22.450 --> 00:08:24.100
For my part, let
me say that when

00:08:24.100 --> 00:08:25.600
it comes to the
role of government

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:29.260
in supporting any aspect
of the academic enterprise,

00:08:29.260 --> 00:08:32.700
I believe that there is
a fundamental principle.

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:36.539
And that is that the free
investigation of ideas, coupled

00:08:36.539 --> 00:08:39.630
with merit review by
knowledgeable experts,

00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:42.659
is essential for maintaining
the intellectual and artistic

00:08:42.659 --> 00:08:47.520
quality and integrity of work
sponsored by outside agencies,

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:49.110
whether they be the
National Science

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:53.320
Foundation or the National
Endowment for the Arts.

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:56.200
This is a principle that we
have had some trouble sustaining

00:08:56.200 --> 00:08:58.630
over the past several
years in our relationship

00:08:58.630 --> 00:09:01.990
with the federal government
in many dimensions.

00:09:01.990 --> 00:09:04.210
Last year's controversy
over the funding

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:07.540
of the Corporal Politics
Exhibit at the List Center

00:09:07.540 --> 00:09:09.590
was but one case in point.

00:09:09.590 --> 00:09:12.160
And I do not want to dwell
on that particular event

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:15.160
because it is just a
single example of the need

00:09:15.160 --> 00:09:18.460
to renew the partnership
between our universities

00:09:18.460 --> 00:09:22.450
and our government, to rebuild
a mutual commitment to investing

00:09:22.450 --> 00:09:26.320
in our long-term future
through education, scholarship,

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:28.000
and research.

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:31.180
The time is ripe for rigorous
and high level examination

00:09:31.180 --> 00:09:33.220
of the federal
government's complex role

00:09:33.220 --> 00:09:35.320
as a patron of the arts.

00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:38.470
This conference provides
an excellent opportunity

00:09:38.470 --> 00:09:41.290
to work together to
delineate the issues

00:09:41.290 --> 00:09:44.230
and articulate our
expectations regarding

00:09:44.230 --> 00:09:47.320
the role of creative
expression in our society

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:50.710
and the role of arts in the
education of our young people.

00:09:50.710 --> 00:09:53.110
I am delighted that
all of you are here,

00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:54.940
and I certainly
look forward eagerly

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:58.091
to the results of
your deliberations.

00:09:58.091 --> 00:10:01.210
Now it is my very great
pleasure to introduce our two

00:10:01.210 --> 00:10:04.880
distinguished keynote
speakers for the evening.

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:06.800
You will hear first
from Senator Edward M.

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:09.530
Kennedy of
Massachusetts, who for 30

00:10:09.530 --> 00:10:12.860
years as a member of the Senate
has played a leading role

00:10:12.860 --> 00:10:15.230
in the affairs of our nation.

00:10:15.230 --> 00:10:17.420
He has been an eloquent
spokesman for the less

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:20.150
privileged in this country
and for human rights

00:10:20.150 --> 00:10:21.830
internationally.

00:10:21.830 --> 00:10:24.560
He certainly has been a
strong and effective advocate

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:28.360
for higher education, for
the advancement of research,

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:32.340
and for the financial support
of needy college students.

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:35.700
He is legendary for his tireless
and fruitful work on behalf

00:10:35.700 --> 00:10:38.160
of the causes he believes in.

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:41.510
Senator Kennedy has chaired
numerous key Senate committees

00:10:41.510 --> 00:10:44.310
and currently is chair of the
Committee on Labor and Human

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:47.550
Resources, which among
its many other duties,

00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:49.830
will oversee the
upcoming reauthorization

00:10:49.830 --> 00:10:54.500
hearings for the National
Endowment for the Arts.

00:10:54.500 --> 00:10:57.890
He will be followed by
Robert Hughes, author and art

00:10:57.890 --> 00:11:01.190
critic who reaches 20
million people a week

00:11:01.190 --> 00:11:04.250
through his reviews
in Time magazine.

00:11:04.250 --> 00:11:07.970
Mr. Hughes is the most widely
read art critic writing today

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:09.990
in the English language.

00:11:09.990 --> 00:11:12.740
He is also a
bestselling historian

00:11:12.740 --> 00:11:16.380
and one of the most popular
art lecturers in America.

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:19.640
His most recent book,
The Culture of Complaint,

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:22.670
is a critique of American
politics and culture

00:11:22.670 --> 00:11:26.390
and has received numerous
enthusiastic reviews.

00:11:26.390 --> 00:11:28.940
I'm sure that his
observations this evening

00:11:28.940 --> 00:11:31.730
will give us much to think
about and to debate over

00:11:31.730 --> 00:11:34.580
the course of this
meeting and beyond.

00:11:34.580 --> 00:11:38.036
First, may I introduce
Senator Kennedy.

00:11:38.036 --> 00:11:42.966
[APPLAUSE]

00:11:47.896 --> 00:11:53.830
KENNEDY: I have to say that's a
little better reception than I

00:11:53.830 --> 00:11:55.240
received the other day.

00:11:55.240 --> 00:11:57.880
I was down in the southern
part of Virginia speaking,

00:11:57.880 --> 00:12:01.750
and someone was introducing me,
and up got some of the people

00:12:01.750 --> 00:12:04.750
in the audience and said
that Kennedys a horse's tail.

00:12:04.750 --> 00:12:05.860
Words to that effect.

00:12:05.860 --> 00:12:09.131
And what happened, some of the
other people at the head table

00:12:09.131 --> 00:12:10.630
dragged this person
out of the room.

00:12:10.630 --> 00:12:14.210
And I said, well, I didn't know
this was a Kennedy country.

00:12:14.210 --> 00:12:15.670
And they said, it isn't.

00:12:15.670 --> 00:12:16.705
It's horse country.

00:12:16.705 --> 00:12:18.840
[LAUGHING]

00:12:18.840 --> 00:12:21.400
I don't want to hear
too much applause.

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:27.640
I want to thank President
Vest for his generous remarks.

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:29.560
And he truly is an
outstanding president

00:12:29.560 --> 00:12:31.570
for this outstanding
institution,

00:12:31.570 --> 00:12:33.900
and I am honored to
be here with him today

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:35.770
for with this
impressive conference.

00:12:35.770 --> 00:12:39.100
And I also want to commend
Ellen Harris, Mark Palmgren,

00:12:39.100 --> 00:12:41.740
and the MIT Office of
the Arts for convening

00:12:41.740 --> 00:12:44.350
at this timely forum
on public policy

00:12:44.350 --> 00:12:46.580
and the federal
role of the arts.

00:12:46.580 --> 00:12:49.220
The arts are a central
part of all of our lives,

00:12:49.220 --> 00:12:52.040
and support for the arts
is an important principle

00:12:52.040 --> 00:12:55.250
of federalism that
I strongly support.

00:12:55.250 --> 00:12:57.230
And it's part of our
national responsibility

00:12:57.230 --> 00:12:59.990
to encourage a climate
in this country that

00:12:59.990 --> 00:13:02.810
promotes development of the arts
and encourages understanding

00:13:02.810 --> 00:13:05.480
and participation in
music, in literature,

00:13:05.480 --> 00:13:07.340
in painting,
sculpture, and dance,

00:13:07.340 --> 00:13:10.400
and many other creative
forms of expression.

00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:13.730
MIT is an ideal venue
for this conference.

00:13:13.730 --> 00:13:15.470
This incomparable
institution has

00:13:15.470 --> 00:13:17.300
produced some of the
finest scientists

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:19.230
and thinkers of our time.

00:13:19.230 --> 00:13:21.500
And it is no coincidence
that MIT also

00:13:21.500 --> 00:13:24.440
has a longstanding policy
that encourages training

00:13:24.440 --> 00:13:27.380
in the arts as a means to
foster creative thinking

00:13:27.380 --> 00:13:30.050
and well-rounded
human development.

00:13:30.050 --> 00:13:31.730
One of your
distinguished professors

00:13:31.730 --> 00:13:34.430
has recently captured
the wonderful blend

00:13:34.430 --> 00:13:37.370
of art and science
in an inspired novel,

00:13:37.370 --> 00:13:39.080
Einstein's Dream.

00:13:39.080 --> 00:13:40.760
It is a warm and
intimate portrait

00:13:40.760 --> 00:13:43.250
of that famous scientist,
and I commend him

00:13:43.250 --> 00:13:46.520
for his achievement,
Professor Alan Lightman.

00:13:46.520 --> 00:13:49.550
It is also a special honor to
share this podium this evening

00:13:49.550 --> 00:13:52.689
with another brilliant writer,
who is also a noted critic.

00:13:52.689 --> 00:13:54.980
I had the honor of meeting
him last year at the Kennedy

00:13:54.980 --> 00:13:58.400
School of Government, and it's
a privilege to be with him now,

00:13:58.400 --> 00:14:00.200
Robert Hughes.

00:14:00.200 --> 00:14:02.450
As some of you
know, or may know,

00:14:02.450 --> 00:14:05.990
I love to paint
with mixed results.

00:14:05.990 --> 00:14:08.480
My artist friends
have long advised

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:11.780
me to stick to politics,
and my Republican colleagues

00:14:11.780 --> 00:14:16.820
in the Senate are urging me to
switch full-time to painting.

00:14:16.820 --> 00:14:20.900
The creative arts represent
our common heritage

00:14:20.900 --> 00:14:23.960
and the universal need
for positive expression.

00:14:23.960 --> 00:14:26.390
They simultaneously
represent the most personal

00:14:26.390 --> 00:14:30.140
of our individual human feelings
and the most universal linking

00:14:30.140 --> 00:14:32.780
us to our common humanity.

00:14:32.780 --> 00:14:34.670
Some nations and
leaders have attempted

00:14:34.670 --> 00:14:38.540
to censor expression they felt
was excessive, or seditious,

00:14:38.540 --> 00:14:40.910
or merely inappropriate.

00:14:40.910 --> 00:14:43.400
But censorship has never
silenced art for long

00:14:43.400 --> 00:14:44.890
in any lab.

00:14:44.890 --> 00:14:47.330
And in a congressional hearing
on the National Endowment

00:14:47.330 --> 00:14:50.600
for the Arts a few years
ago, the great conductor

00:14:50.600 --> 00:14:52.970
Rostropovich spoke
eloquently of the artists

00:14:52.970 --> 00:14:55.880
in his Russian homeland,
who lived with censorship

00:14:55.880 --> 00:14:58.370
and sometimes lived in exile.

00:14:58.370 --> 00:15:00.550
Creative expression
still endured.

00:15:00.550 --> 00:15:04.550
The great composers, such as
Shostakovich and Prokofiev,

00:15:04.550 --> 00:15:06.510
composed wonderful works.

00:15:06.510 --> 00:15:09.680
But as Rostropovich sadly
noted, their fellow citizens

00:15:09.680 --> 00:15:11.720
were never permitted
by their government

00:15:11.720 --> 00:15:14.900
to share the joy of
these masterpieces.

00:15:14.900 --> 00:15:17.900
It is remarkable and instructive
that the leader who guided

00:15:17.900 --> 00:15:21.680
Czechoslovakia out of its dark
years of oppression was a great

00:15:21.680 --> 00:15:25.280
writer and a visionary,
Václav Havel.

00:15:25.280 --> 00:15:30.050
The soul of a nation cannot
be extinguished by any censor.

00:15:30.050 --> 00:15:34.280
It is not only foolhardy,
but futile for politicians

00:15:34.280 --> 00:15:37.740
to attempt to write
guidelines for acceptable art.

00:15:37.740 --> 00:15:42.590
The arts defy orthodoxy or
any other form of mediocrity.

00:15:42.590 --> 00:15:45.980
Regrettably, in recent years,
the most public discussion

00:15:45.980 --> 00:15:48.170
of the arts in this
country has been

00:15:48.170 --> 00:15:52.220
the debate over controversial
works supported by the National

00:15:52.220 --> 00:15:53.510
Endowment.

00:15:53.510 --> 00:15:56.780
This debate misstates the
mission of the endowment

00:15:56.780 --> 00:15:59.540
and insults its proud
record of achievement

00:15:59.540 --> 00:16:01.960
over the past quarter century.

00:16:01.960 --> 00:16:06.130
It takes a very narrow-minded
critic to see the molehill

00:16:06.130 --> 00:16:07.840
and miss the mountain.

00:16:07.840 --> 00:16:11.290
The arts endowment does not
belong to any one person

00:16:11.290 --> 00:16:13.370
or political party.

00:16:13.370 --> 00:16:16.510
It reflects the national
spirit and broad diversity

00:16:16.510 --> 00:16:17.890
in our country.

00:16:17.890 --> 00:16:21.220
It always has, and
it always should.

00:16:21.220 --> 00:16:24.340
But the endowment is
a fragile institution.

00:16:24.340 --> 00:16:26.890
It will survive only as
long as the peer panel

00:16:26.890 --> 00:16:30.550
system, not political
second guessing system,

00:16:30.550 --> 00:16:35.020
determines which applications
for support are funded.

00:16:35.020 --> 00:16:37.910
Artistic excellence,
not political passion,

00:16:37.910 --> 00:16:40.250
must guide these decisions.

00:16:40.250 --> 00:16:43.270
Occasional mistakes should
not become the pretext

00:16:43.270 --> 00:16:48.100
for destroying its noble mission
or hamstringing its procedures.

00:16:48.100 --> 00:16:49.870
The great irony
of this debate is

00:16:49.870 --> 00:16:53.710
that what Senator Jesse Helms
and his indecency brigade

00:16:53.710 --> 00:16:57.812
want to do with Soviet
ties American art.

00:16:57.812 --> 00:17:00.680
As all of you know,
the List Center

00:17:00.680 --> 00:17:02.660
became part of a
national controversy

00:17:02.660 --> 00:17:05.660
when an application to
support an MIT exhibition

00:17:05.660 --> 00:17:07.940
was rejected by the endowment.

00:17:07.940 --> 00:17:10.790
And to your credit and
the endowment's discredit,

00:17:10.790 --> 00:17:13.280
the show went on
with private support

00:17:13.280 --> 00:17:16.040
replacing the federal
support it deserved.

00:17:16.040 --> 00:17:19.250
Incredibly, the acting
chairman of the endowment

00:17:19.250 --> 00:17:23.210
subverted her agency's
own procedures.

00:17:23.210 --> 00:17:25.700
She ignored the recommendation
of the National Council

00:17:25.700 --> 00:17:28.430
on the Arts and the
peer review system,

00:17:28.430 --> 00:17:31.130
steps established
by statute to avoid

00:17:31.130 --> 00:17:33.500
just this type of
political intrusion

00:17:33.500 --> 00:17:36.380
into the endowment's
deliberation.

00:17:36.380 --> 00:17:40.020
A national conference like
this is an appropriate forum

00:17:40.020 --> 00:17:43.980
to state and restate some
fundamental principles

00:17:43.980 --> 00:17:46.350
and avoid a repetition
of distressing incidents

00:17:46.350 --> 00:17:47.730
like that.

00:17:47.730 --> 00:17:53.070
In a time of other urgent needs
and few resources to meet them,

00:17:53.070 --> 00:17:56.490
some conclude that we cannot
afford to support the arts,

00:17:56.490 --> 00:17:58.940
but that view is short-sighted.

00:17:58.940 --> 00:18:01.130
Federal support for the
arts is an investment

00:18:01.130 --> 00:18:03.770
in the future that will
bring important benefits

00:18:03.770 --> 00:18:06.800
to the quality of life
in our communities.

00:18:06.800 --> 00:18:10.640
A recent national poll found
that fully 92% of Americans

00:18:10.640 --> 00:18:12.950
say that the arts are
important to the life

00:18:12.950 --> 00:18:14.300
of their neighborhoods.

00:18:14.300 --> 00:18:18.530
And they are also important
to their economies.

00:18:18.530 --> 00:18:20.450
Ticket sales to
performing arts events

00:18:20.450 --> 00:18:24.860
surpass spectator sports by
over a billion dollars in 1989.

00:18:24.860 --> 00:18:28.510
In Charleston, South Carolina,
the Spoleto USA Festival

00:18:28.510 --> 00:18:30.950
has brought $30 million a year
to the city in the past 10

00:18:30.950 --> 00:18:31.930
years.

00:18:31.930 --> 00:18:34.820
And in Massachusetts, the impact
has been just as dramatic.

00:18:34.820 --> 00:18:38.810
In 1987 and '91, state and
federal funds for the arts

00:18:38.810 --> 00:18:41.150
totalled almost
$120 million dollars

00:18:41.150 --> 00:18:44.150
and reached over 200
million people and 1 and 1/2

00:18:44.150 --> 00:18:46.170
million artists.

00:18:46.170 --> 00:18:50.130
Federal support provides
the essential seed money

00:18:50.130 --> 00:18:53.250
for broader state and
local private aid.

00:18:53.250 --> 00:18:56.850
Nationwide, the federal
investment is less than $0.70

00:18:56.850 --> 00:18:59.090
a citizen a year.

00:18:59.090 --> 00:19:01.370
Arguably, it's one of
the best investments

00:19:01.370 --> 00:19:03.730
the federal government can make.

00:19:03.730 --> 00:19:07.400
Our artists are also our most
visible goodwill ambassadors

00:19:07.400 --> 00:19:08.300
abroad.

00:19:08.300 --> 00:19:11.540
Aaron Copland, Leonard
Bernstein, Agnes de Mille,

00:19:11.540 --> 00:19:13.880
and Martha Graham
are quintessentially

00:19:13.880 --> 00:19:17.580
American artists, but
their appeal is worldwide.

00:19:17.580 --> 00:19:19.925
And so too Ernest Hemingway,
and Emily Dickinson,

00:19:19.925 --> 00:19:23.210
and William Faulkner are great
American writers whose words

00:19:23.210 --> 00:19:26.000
reach out and touch the world.

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:28.610
The appeal of our artists
was dramatically demonstrated

00:19:28.610 --> 00:19:30.980
on inauguration
day last January,

00:19:30.980 --> 00:19:35.090
when Maya Angelou recited her
a beautiful poem celebrating

00:19:35.090 --> 00:19:38.240
the American national spirit,
and the sense of renewal

00:19:38.240 --> 00:19:43.910
that all of us felt that day in
her 1972 autobiography volume,

00:19:43.910 --> 00:19:47.900
I Know Why the Caged Bird Sings
returned to the best seller

00:19:47.900 --> 00:19:50.600
list and has been
there ever since.

00:19:50.600 --> 00:19:52.400
A miracle of marketing?

00:19:52.400 --> 00:19:53.190
No.

00:19:53.190 --> 00:19:55.310
A miracle of art.

00:19:55.310 --> 00:19:58.680
President Kennedy understood
the importance of the arts.

00:19:58.680 --> 00:20:00.230
The Center for the
Performing Arts

00:20:00.230 --> 00:20:01.850
that bears his
name in Washington

00:20:01.850 --> 00:20:05.060
is a fitting living
memorial to his vision.

00:20:05.060 --> 00:20:07.010
On its walls are
carved his words,

00:20:07.010 --> 00:20:11.300
which symbolize the hope he had
for our country and his belief

00:20:11.300 --> 00:20:13.610
that we reach the
highest when we

00:20:13.610 --> 00:20:17.340
reach deepest within ourselves.

00:20:17.340 --> 00:20:19.860
I am certain that after
the dust of centuries

00:20:19.860 --> 00:20:22.770
has passed over
our cities, we too

00:20:22.770 --> 00:20:26.280
will be remembered not for our
victories or defeats in battle

00:20:26.280 --> 00:20:29.280
or in politics, but
for our contribution

00:20:29.280 --> 00:20:31.930
to the human spirit.

00:20:31.930 --> 00:20:37.200
The arts chronicle our
history, record our successes,

00:20:37.200 --> 00:20:41.330
warn of our weaknesses,
expand our understanding.

00:20:41.330 --> 00:20:46.420
They challenge us, not just to
do better, but to do our best.

00:20:46.420 --> 00:20:49.420
Federal support to
institutions and individuals

00:20:49.420 --> 00:20:51.310
must be accompanied
by accountability

00:20:51.310 --> 00:20:54.850
like any other federal program,
but federal support should not

00:20:54.850 --> 00:20:57.880
be conditioned or
encumbered by restrictions

00:20:57.880 --> 00:21:00.070
that interfere with
artistic freedom

00:21:00.070 --> 00:21:02.890
any more than any
federal aid to education

00:21:02.890 --> 00:21:06.080
should be used to interfere
with academic freedom.

00:21:06.080 --> 00:21:09.710
In testimony before Congress,
constitutional scholar Kathleen

00:21:09.710 --> 00:21:14.510
Sullivan warned against content
restrictions on federal aid

00:21:14.510 --> 00:21:15.890
to the arts.

00:21:15.890 --> 00:21:19.080
We are each masters
in our own homes.

00:21:19.080 --> 00:21:21.560
There we may pick and
choose the ideas to which

00:21:21.560 --> 00:21:24.440
we open our doors or minds.

00:21:24.440 --> 00:21:28.850
But in the government's
house, all ideas are welcome.

00:21:28.850 --> 00:21:32.330
Sometimes art shocks
our sensibilities.

00:21:32.330 --> 00:21:34.130
It is supposed to.

00:21:34.130 --> 00:21:37.400
But that is no justification
for unwarranted intrusions

00:21:37.400 --> 00:21:39.860
by extremists who, if
the truth be known,

00:21:39.860 --> 00:21:42.510
oppose any federal
aid to the arts.

00:21:42.510 --> 00:21:45.270
Obscenity is the place
to draw the line.

00:21:45.270 --> 00:21:47.160
Federal dollars
should not be spent

00:21:47.160 --> 00:21:49.620
on any project that is obscene.

00:21:49.620 --> 00:21:53.130
Current law explicitly prohibits
funding for such projects.

00:21:53.130 --> 00:21:55.050
If funds are awarded
for such purposes,

00:21:55.050 --> 00:21:56.770
they must be refunded.

00:21:56.770 --> 00:21:58.500
The proper way to
determine obscenity

00:21:58.500 --> 00:22:00.120
is through the courts.

00:22:00.120 --> 00:22:04.320
A new round of contentious
debate about these principles

00:22:04.320 --> 00:22:06.780
is on the horizon in Congress.

00:22:06.780 --> 00:22:11.100
The Senate must confirm a new
head of the Arts Endowment,

00:22:11.100 --> 00:22:15.450
and we must pass new authorizing
legislation for the agency.

00:22:15.450 --> 00:22:18.120
Congress will consider
a two-year extension

00:22:18.120 --> 00:22:20.940
of the endowment in
the months ahead.

00:22:20.940 --> 00:22:23.640
This short-term action will
permit the new administration

00:22:23.640 --> 00:22:26.660
to install a new
team at the endowment

00:22:26.660 --> 00:22:30.720
and to participate fully
in a comprehensive debate

00:22:30.720 --> 00:22:32.610
about these issues.

00:22:32.610 --> 00:22:34.620
By any fair accounting,
the endowment

00:22:34.620 --> 00:22:37.630
has fulfilled its mission.

00:22:37.630 --> 00:22:41.670
It has done an exceptional
job in providing support

00:22:41.670 --> 00:22:44.130
to the nation's
cultural institutions,

00:22:44.130 --> 00:22:47.880
and it has dramatically
increased the people's access

00:22:47.880 --> 00:22:49.230
to the arts.

00:22:49.230 --> 00:22:52.560
The challenge for both the
administration and the Congress

00:22:52.560 --> 00:22:55.560
is to guide the endowment
beyond the current criticism

00:22:55.560 --> 00:22:57.090
and controversy.

00:22:57.090 --> 00:22:59.070
The past administration
was willing to be

00:22:59.070 --> 00:23:03.030
part of the problem rather
than seek a fair solution.

00:23:03.030 --> 00:23:05.490
With the new administration,
we have an excellent chance

00:23:05.490 --> 00:23:08.100
to restore confidence
in the agency

00:23:08.100 --> 00:23:11.640
and provide a new sense of
purpose for its mission.

00:23:11.640 --> 00:23:15.990
Now is not the time, nor I hope
will there ever come a time,

00:23:15.990 --> 00:23:18.900
to turn our back on this
essential public commitment

00:23:18.900 --> 00:23:21.480
to the arts and free expression.

00:23:21.480 --> 00:23:23.280
In closing, let me
say again that it

00:23:23.280 --> 00:23:25.630
is an honor to be a
part of this conference.

00:23:25.630 --> 00:23:28.260
I look forward to the
results of your deliberation,

00:23:28.260 --> 00:23:30.270
and I'm grateful to
you for including me

00:23:30.270 --> 00:23:32.110
in this important discussion.

00:23:32.110 --> 00:23:33.783
Thank you.

00:23:33.783 --> 00:23:38.693
[APPLAUSE]

00:23:55.387 --> 00:23:59.110
HUGHES: Well, ladies
and gentlemen,

00:23:59.110 --> 00:24:01.150
when Mr. Clinton
and the Democrats

00:24:01.150 --> 00:24:03.220
came to power in Washington
a few months ago,

00:24:03.220 --> 00:24:05.530
there was a good deal
of optimistic talk

00:24:05.530 --> 00:24:08.470
among the chattering
classes, to which I belong,

00:24:08.470 --> 00:24:12.760
about the end of ideology
in American politics.

00:24:12.760 --> 00:24:15.010
After the bruising
divisions of the '80s,

00:24:15.010 --> 00:24:17.740
a time of healing
characterized by something

00:24:17.740 --> 00:24:22.550
called a search for the center
was supposed to be upon us.

00:24:22.550 --> 00:24:25.280
The alternative, which
was set forth in a speech

00:24:25.280 --> 00:24:27.200
to the Republican
Convention in Houston

00:24:27.200 --> 00:24:32.330
by Pat Buchanan, the
only man of my generation

00:24:32.330 --> 00:24:36.450
who ever made me feel however
briefly ashamed to be Irish--

00:24:36.450 --> 00:24:38.400
[APPLAUSE]

00:24:43.800 --> 00:24:46.290
Well, the alternative was
just too ugly to contemplate.

00:24:46.290 --> 00:24:49.300
They have a rhetoric of
revenge, of cultural war.

00:24:49.300 --> 00:24:52.770
Remember they're taking
America back street by street

00:24:52.770 --> 00:24:54.480
from those who were
not really American,

00:24:54.480 --> 00:24:58.620
such as blacks and homosexuals
and liberals and atheists.

00:24:58.620 --> 00:25:01.260
Now, Bush that
allowed his program

00:25:01.260 --> 00:25:03.480
to be written by the right
wing of his own party,

00:25:03.480 --> 00:25:05.670
unfortunately, which
staked everything

00:25:05.670 --> 00:25:09.510
upon the idea of a cultural
and religious struggle.

00:25:09.510 --> 00:25:11.670
Winning it was going
to return America

00:25:11.670 --> 00:25:15.150
to its original virtues, which
had been in steady decline

00:25:15.150 --> 00:25:18.030
despite nearly 15 years of
Republican rule since the New

00:25:18.030 --> 00:25:19.770
Deal.

00:25:19.770 --> 00:25:23.400
And once the Republicans
had lost many liberals

00:25:23.400 --> 00:25:26.460
hoped that these specters
of ideological intolerance

00:25:26.460 --> 00:25:29.250
would begin to retreat,
baffled and defeated

00:25:29.250 --> 00:25:31.380
from the field of culture.

00:25:31.380 --> 00:25:34.710
Haven't they tried to fight
an election on cultural issues

00:25:34.710 --> 00:25:36.360
and lost?

00:25:36.360 --> 00:25:39.360
Wouldn't they conclude from
this that America's overriding

00:25:39.360 --> 00:25:41.860
problem might indeed
be the economy, stupid,

00:25:41.860 --> 00:25:44.730
and not the issues
so richly symbolized

00:25:44.730 --> 00:25:47.010
in the various attacks
upon the National Endowment

00:25:47.010 --> 00:25:50.590
for the Arts that were
launched in the late '80s

00:25:50.590 --> 00:25:53.720
Well, the short answer is no.

00:25:53.720 --> 00:25:56.680
There is no sign of an end
to ideological division

00:25:56.680 --> 00:25:58.870
in America, and
still less is there

00:25:58.870 --> 00:26:03.910
any sign of healing in the
field of culture and the arts.

00:26:03.910 --> 00:26:06.040
In fact, the splits
only seem to get

00:26:06.040 --> 00:26:09.920
wider and the shuffle
across them louder.

00:26:09.920 --> 00:26:12.290
Jesse Helms and his
ilk spent a few months

00:26:12.290 --> 00:26:14.930
licking their wounds,
but now, rest assured,

00:26:14.930 --> 00:26:17.780
they are licking their
chops, and they will be

00:26:17.780 --> 00:26:21.110
back and with reinforcements.

00:26:21.110 --> 00:26:24.990
What happened to the
NEA of the late '80s

00:26:24.990 --> 00:26:26.930
was probably only
a prelude to what

00:26:26.930 --> 00:26:29.360
is likely to happen in
the immediate future

00:26:29.360 --> 00:26:31.910
and a few years down the
road as the millennial year

00:26:31.910 --> 00:26:34.100
2000 approaches.

00:26:34.100 --> 00:26:36.770
The morals of public
funding is too harsh

00:26:36.770 --> 00:26:41.120
and too handy a baton to
remain unprecedented for long.

00:26:41.120 --> 00:26:43.700
Now thus far, nobody
has been appointed

00:26:43.700 --> 00:26:46.070
as the new director
of the NEA, though we

00:26:46.070 --> 00:26:48.500
don't know who
it's likely to be,

00:26:48.500 --> 00:26:51.530
nor do I know what the
president's cultural policies

00:26:51.530 --> 00:26:53.690
are, in fact, likely to be.

00:26:53.690 --> 00:26:56.120
I earnestly pray that
we cannot deduce them

00:26:56.120 --> 00:26:57.980
for the inauguration
festivities.

00:27:04.441 --> 00:27:05.940
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:05.940 --> 00:27:08.540
Stage managed by a couple
of sitcom producers

00:27:08.540 --> 00:27:10.800
and stuffed with
Hollywood celebs,

00:27:10.800 --> 00:27:13.140
with the sole concession
to high culture

00:27:13.140 --> 00:27:16.020
being a poem by Maya
Angelou, on whose merits

00:27:16.020 --> 00:27:19.560
I disagree with Senator Kennedy.

00:27:19.560 --> 00:27:23.220
The most urgent one, however,
its most urgent priority,

00:27:23.220 --> 00:27:26.460
it seems to me, ought to
be defending the endowment.

00:27:26.460 --> 00:27:28.680
Well, one of the
most priorities ought

00:27:28.680 --> 00:27:31.800
to be defending the endowment
against the pressures of both

00:27:31.800 --> 00:27:34.380
the right and the
left in America.

00:27:34.380 --> 00:27:36.030
If there was ever a
time when we needed

00:27:36.030 --> 00:27:38.100
to think freely and
openly about what

00:27:38.100 --> 00:27:41.820
the National Endowment for
the Arts can and can't do,

00:27:41.820 --> 00:27:44.420
it is now.

00:27:44.420 --> 00:27:47.450
The NEA it is an institution
unique to America.

00:27:47.450 --> 00:27:49.850
It is also quite young.

00:27:49.850 --> 00:27:52.800
From the Declaration of
Independence until the 1960s,

00:27:52.800 --> 00:27:55.280
there had been
absolutely no tradition

00:27:55.280 --> 00:27:58.100
of federal support for
the arts in this country,

00:27:58.100 --> 00:28:00.580
although there had been
intermittent stabs at it.

00:28:00.580 --> 00:28:03.500
And where federal money
and arts policy crossed,

00:28:03.500 --> 00:28:06.110
there were usually long delays
and quite a lot of rancor

00:28:06.110 --> 00:28:07.730
and a tremendous
amount of log rolling

00:28:07.730 --> 00:28:11.250
and the opening of
many pork barrel.

00:28:11.250 --> 00:28:13.280
Controversy, for instance,
over the monument

00:28:13.280 --> 00:28:16.160
to George Washington in the 19th
century, which took something

00:28:16.160 --> 00:28:19.190
like 70 years to
build, was as fierce

00:28:19.190 --> 00:28:23.930
as it was over Maya Lin's
Vietnam memorial in the 20th.

00:28:23.930 --> 00:28:26.480
Traditional American
laissez faire left no room

00:28:26.480 --> 00:28:28.569
for federal arts subsidies.

00:28:28.569 --> 00:28:30.110
After all, why should
it in a country

00:28:30.110 --> 00:28:33.230
that also rejected such
novelties as public housing

00:28:33.230 --> 00:28:36.350
or health care or
unemployment insurance?

00:28:36.350 --> 00:28:39.410
City money went to the
construction of civic centers

00:28:39.410 --> 00:28:44.780
between 1900 and 1950, and there
were significant tax exemptions

00:28:44.780 --> 00:28:47.640
for donors, for concert
halls, for museums,

00:28:47.640 --> 00:28:50.600
but active funding of
the arts from Washington

00:28:50.600 --> 00:28:52.790
was kept to the
very bare minimum.

00:28:52.790 --> 00:28:56.960
As a result, what was
performed, exhibited, and played

00:28:56.960 --> 00:28:59.690
in American institutions
between the wars

00:28:59.690 --> 00:29:04.660
was largely Europe-dominated
and Europe-driven.

00:29:04.660 --> 00:29:06.625
Now as James Heilbrun
and Charles Gray

00:29:06.625 --> 00:29:09.460
have pointed out in their
book, The Economics of Art

00:29:09.460 --> 00:29:12.580
and Culture, which is due from
Cambridge University Press,

00:29:12.580 --> 00:29:16.270
I believe, in August and is
the best introduction that I

00:29:16.270 --> 00:29:21.080
know to the labyrinth of the
economics of American culture,

00:29:21.080 --> 00:29:25.570
this situation began to change
in the 1950s, when the Ford

00:29:25.570 --> 00:29:29.140
Foundation began a program of
grants for American symphony

00:29:29.140 --> 00:29:30.190
orchestras.

00:29:30.190 --> 00:29:32.920
But the big push for
large scale funding

00:29:32.920 --> 00:29:35.260
of the arts from state
and federal governments

00:29:35.260 --> 00:29:37.210
started in the early '60s.

00:29:37.210 --> 00:29:40.300
At the state level, it began
as many of you will recall,

00:29:40.300 --> 00:29:43.480
when Governor Nelson Rockefeller
set up the New York State

00:29:43.480 --> 00:29:47.410
Council on the Arts, whose
funding was to peek at in 1976

00:29:47.410 --> 00:29:51.400
at $35 million a year.

00:29:51.400 --> 00:29:52.840
The idea of the
National Endowment

00:29:52.840 --> 00:29:56.770
for the Arts, the NEA,
was proposed in 1961

00:29:56.770 --> 00:29:59.170
under John Kennedy
by August Heckscher,

00:29:59.170 --> 00:30:02.260
and Lyndon Johnson pushed
it through after Kennedy's

00:30:02.260 --> 00:30:03.970
assassination.

00:30:03.970 --> 00:30:07.360
The twin sister agencies,
the NEA and the NEH

00:30:07.360 --> 00:30:09.790
were written into law in 1965.

00:30:09.790 --> 00:30:13.330
And then, as now, these
agencies were strongly

00:30:13.330 --> 00:30:16.270
opposed in Congress
by Southern Democrats

00:30:16.270 --> 00:30:17.750
and conservative Republicans.

00:30:17.750 --> 00:30:20.020
There was in those
days actually a group

00:30:20.020 --> 00:30:22.990
of people called
conservative Republicans.

00:30:22.990 --> 00:30:26.380
There never has been a
consensus on the need

00:30:26.380 --> 00:30:29.137
for national cultural
endowments in America.

00:30:29.137 --> 00:30:30.970
And the arguments against
them have scarcely

00:30:30.970 --> 00:30:33.790
changed in 50 years.

00:30:33.790 --> 00:30:35.620
They lead to government
control of culture,

00:30:35.620 --> 00:30:39.100
which tastes of socialism,
or else promotes mediocrity.

00:30:39.100 --> 00:30:40.660
They cost too much.

00:30:40.660 --> 00:30:43.930
Their worth can't be
shown on a balance sheet.

00:30:43.930 --> 00:30:47.080
They reduce the virtues
of private patronage.

00:30:47.080 --> 00:30:50.620
More recently-- and this
is argued from controversy

00:30:50.620 --> 00:30:53.200
over five or six grunts
among the tens of thousands

00:30:53.200 --> 00:30:56.650
made by the NEA in
the last 30 years,

00:30:56.650 --> 00:31:00.700
they spread filth
and immorality.

00:31:00.700 --> 00:31:04.270
Now, right from
the start, the NEA

00:31:04.270 --> 00:31:07.930
was only interested in
fostering the production

00:31:07.930 --> 00:31:11.350
of new works of art and in
contemporary institutional

00:31:11.350 --> 00:31:12.490
practice.

00:31:12.490 --> 00:31:14.900
Methods of historical
preservation,

00:31:14.900 --> 00:31:17.080
which are very much to
the fore in the thinking

00:31:17.080 --> 00:31:20.230
of cultural agencies
in European countries,

00:31:20.230 --> 00:31:24.900
were not within its purview,
as they are, for instance,

00:31:24.900 --> 00:31:27.560
within the purview of
the Ministries of Culture

00:31:27.560 --> 00:31:30.280
of France, or Germany, or Italy.

00:31:30.280 --> 00:31:34.180
The NEA's budget began in
'65 at $10 million a year,

00:31:34.180 --> 00:31:37.630
and it reached its peak of
effective spending power, which

00:31:37.630 --> 00:31:41.460
was $150 million, in 1979.

00:31:41.460 --> 00:31:43.640
And though, the
nominal budget has

00:31:43.640 --> 00:31:45.850
gone as high as $180
million in the years

00:31:45.850 --> 00:31:48.680
since, when you
figure in inflation,

00:31:48.680 --> 00:31:53.750
the present spending power of
the NEA is somewhere around 40%

00:31:53.750 --> 00:31:57.560
below its actual
value 15 years ago.

00:31:57.560 --> 00:32:02.015
It is about what one
Japanese collector, a forest

00:32:02.015 --> 00:32:06.725
tripper called Mr. Saito,
paid for two paintings, a Van

00:32:06.725 --> 00:32:11.990
Gogh and a Renoir, just before
the art market crash in 1990.

00:32:11.990 --> 00:32:14.780
It is also less per
capita, actually,

00:32:14.780 --> 00:32:19.220
than the money given in
cultural support by the States.

00:32:19.220 --> 00:32:21.020
Now this, compared
to other countries,

00:32:21.020 --> 00:32:26.530
was and is a tiny, a minuscule,
a most parsimonious subsidy.

00:32:26.530 --> 00:32:32.410
A study done by Mark Schuster
for the NEA in '85 showed that

00:32:32.410 --> 00:32:36.160
America was then giving $3 per
capita to the arts in direct

00:32:36.160 --> 00:32:40.330
federal and state owed,
as against $35 in Sweden,

00:32:40.330 --> 00:32:45.610
$32 in Canada and France, and
so on down to $9.60 in Margaret

00:32:45.610 --> 00:32:48.059
Thatcher's Great Britain.

00:32:48.059 --> 00:32:49.600
However, this wasn't
the whole story,

00:32:49.600 --> 00:32:51.349
of course, because
none of these countries

00:32:51.349 --> 00:32:53.860
gave significant indirect
support to the arts

00:32:53.860 --> 00:32:58.450
by allowing business or personal
tax deductions for gifts.

00:32:58.450 --> 00:33:02.890
Factor those in, and American
support went up to somewhere

00:33:02.890 --> 00:33:07.510
around about $13 per capita,
above Great Britain, certainly,

00:33:07.510 --> 00:33:10.900
but still below all the rest.

00:33:10.900 --> 00:33:14.730
For there is a further
component, which wholly depends

00:33:14.730 --> 00:33:18.240
upon the NEA, which is the
matching grant, by which

00:33:18.240 --> 00:33:21.100
the NEA puts it, as it
were, has often been said,

00:33:21.100 --> 00:33:23.280
it's a good housekeeping
seal of approval

00:33:23.280 --> 00:33:25.500
on a project by
giving half the money,

00:33:25.500 --> 00:33:28.320
and thus inducing another
sponsor, corporate one

00:33:28.320 --> 00:33:31.140
or whatever, to
pony up the rest.

00:33:31.140 --> 00:33:34.860
The NEA is now and
always has functioned

00:33:34.860 --> 00:33:38.880
as a vital catalyst for
non-government money

00:33:38.880 --> 00:33:40.050
for the arts.

00:33:40.050 --> 00:33:44.700
And there seems to me to
be no convincing evidence

00:33:44.700 --> 00:33:47.580
that what conservatives claim,
namely that private money would

00:33:47.580 --> 00:33:50.880
rush in to fill the gap if the
NEA were to go out of business,

00:33:50.880 --> 00:33:54.370
is true or even
remotely probable.

00:33:54.370 --> 00:33:58.590
For as a rule, corporate
money likes prestige ventures,

00:33:58.590 --> 00:34:02.340
proven successors, wants to
know in advance that it's going

00:34:02.340 --> 00:34:04.590
to be a Broadway hit,
whereas it's essential

00:34:04.590 --> 00:34:07.710
to the NEA's mandate, as it was
conceived and as I believe it

00:34:07.710 --> 00:34:12.690
should continue to go, to
care for what is unproven,

00:34:12.690 --> 00:34:19.230
for what cannot yet succeed
necessarily in the marketplace.

00:34:19.230 --> 00:34:21.630
You can contrast the position
of the head of the NEA

00:34:21.630 --> 00:34:23.880
with that of Monsieur
Jacques Toubon, who

00:34:23.880 --> 00:34:26.144
is the new Minister
of Culture in France.

00:34:28.989 --> 00:34:30.850
Toubon actually wanted today--

00:34:30.850 --> 00:34:33.130
he's a lawyer, and he wanted
the Ministry of Justice,

00:34:33.130 --> 00:34:34.929
but his wife has an
interest in the arts,

00:34:34.929 --> 00:34:37.360
and so he settled for
the Ministry of Culture,

00:34:37.360 --> 00:34:40.570
in which position he
disposes of an annual budget

00:34:40.570 --> 00:34:46.000
of approximately $2 billion, 100
times the American equivalent,

00:34:46.000 --> 00:34:48.520
with which he can, commission
works of art for the state,

00:34:48.520 --> 00:34:50.620
and restore Gothic
churches, and defend

00:34:50.620 --> 00:34:53.590
the purity of the French
language, a prospect which

00:34:53.590 --> 00:34:55.300
I guess would give
nightmares to anybody

00:34:55.300 --> 00:34:59.230
who tried the same thing
in Washington today,

00:34:59.230 --> 00:35:02.470
to build opera houses, and
to underwrite the French film

00:35:02.470 --> 00:35:06.130
industry with the help
of a tax of 17.5%, which

00:35:06.130 --> 00:35:08.810
is applied to every ticket
to an American movie that's

00:35:08.810 --> 00:35:11.050
sold in France.

00:35:11.050 --> 00:35:13.570
And it is in this way
that Arnold Schwarzenegger

00:35:13.570 --> 00:35:16.268
plays for Gerard Depardieu.

00:35:16.268 --> 00:35:20.720
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:20.720 --> 00:35:23.210
He stands at the top of
a pyramid of interlocking

00:35:23.210 --> 00:35:25.790
cultural interest, which
resembles the court of Louis

00:35:25.790 --> 00:35:28.220
the 14th, except
that it's much bigger

00:35:28.220 --> 00:35:31.010
and has been magnified
over the last 20 years

00:35:31.010 --> 00:35:35.120
by the requirements of Francois
Mitterrand and Jack Lang's

00:35:35.120 --> 00:35:37.610
Champagne Socialism.

00:35:37.610 --> 00:35:41.300
A state-subsidized
composer, official composer,

00:35:41.300 --> 00:35:44.960
like Pierre Boulez,
is as a result by far

00:35:44.960 --> 00:35:47.750
the most indulged and
politically powerful

00:35:47.750 --> 00:35:51.200
man probably in the entire
history of musical politics

00:35:51.200 --> 00:35:52.460
in France or anywhere else.

00:35:52.460 --> 00:35:54.360
To compare his
influence to that say,

00:35:54.360 --> 00:35:57.740
of Philip Glass or the
late John Cage in America

00:35:57.740 --> 00:36:01.430
is like comparing
ants and elephants.

00:36:01.430 --> 00:36:05.000
As Benjamin Disraeli
observed, if you

00:36:05.000 --> 00:36:09.620
want to know what envy is,
you must live among artists.

00:36:09.620 --> 00:36:16.460
And if one remembers the
words of a 19th century French

00:36:16.460 --> 00:36:19.010
Minister of Culture, who a
night out after a particularly

00:36:19.010 --> 00:36:23.120
trying day in the office,
remarked that every time I

00:36:23.120 --> 00:36:28.670
award a commission, I create
one ingrate and 30 malcontents.

00:36:28.670 --> 00:36:31.610
Such are the joys of
patronage, but what

00:36:31.610 --> 00:36:35.450
would make even sponsorship holy
impractical, or particularly

00:36:35.450 --> 00:36:39.320
on the NEA's tiny budget, is the
conservative agenda, the agenda

00:36:39.320 --> 00:36:42.290
of some conservatives
at least, of prevetting

00:36:42.290 --> 00:36:46.010
all grounds for the moral
content of their supposed

00:36:46.010 --> 00:36:47.450
results.

00:36:47.450 --> 00:36:48.654
Apart from the difficulty--

00:36:48.654 --> 00:36:50.570
I mean, as it were, the
existential difficulty

00:36:50.570 --> 00:36:55.010
of censoring a work before
it is actually made,

00:36:55.010 --> 00:36:58.460
modern democracy, I think,
cannot contemplate without

00:36:58.460 --> 00:37:01.510
revulsion the machinery of
supervision that this would

00:37:01.510 --> 00:37:03.206
entail.

00:37:03.206 --> 00:37:05.330
As a matter of fact, neither
can many conservatives

00:37:05.330 --> 00:37:06.746
imagine it, which
is why they say,

00:37:06.746 --> 00:37:09.020
you should just
get rid of the NEA.

00:37:09.020 --> 00:37:11.810
Better no sponsorship at all
than the risk of blasphemy

00:37:11.810 --> 00:37:14.480
or battery on government money.

00:37:14.480 --> 00:37:16.940
In fact, many
conservatives seem to me

00:37:16.940 --> 00:37:19.250
to be caught in a
sort of contradiction

00:37:19.250 --> 00:37:21.704
on cultural subsidies,
half of them saying

00:37:21.704 --> 00:37:23.870
that the national endowment
should fold because it's

00:37:23.870 --> 00:37:26.900
elitist, and the other half
claiming that in the end,

00:37:26.900 --> 00:37:28.490
only high culture
is worth having

00:37:28.490 --> 00:37:30.380
and that the NEA should
go because it doesn't

00:37:30.380 --> 00:37:32.920
aim high enough.

00:37:32.920 --> 00:37:35.950
The taxpayers' money
question is, of course,

00:37:35.950 --> 00:37:38.280
an extremely thorny one.

00:37:38.280 --> 00:37:41.060
The American right likes
to brandish it as though it

00:37:41.060 --> 00:37:43.470
were completely unanswerable.

00:37:43.470 --> 00:37:45.950
And the difficulty for
the left and the center

00:37:45.950 --> 00:37:48.800
is that they've used it so often
themselves against government

00:37:48.800 --> 00:37:50.590
spending that they
don't like, you

00:37:50.590 --> 00:37:53.300
know, such as military waste,
if your sense of frugality

00:37:53.300 --> 00:37:57.530
is offended by the famous $1,000
ratchet wrench or the $600

00:37:57.530 --> 00:38:01.040
toilet seat in a bomber,
why shouldn't someone else

00:38:01.040 --> 00:38:03.350
raise a hue and
cry about $40,000

00:38:03.350 --> 00:38:06.050
worth of NEA money going
to subsidize a photography

00:38:06.050 --> 00:38:08.900
show that includes a picture
of the artist with a bullwhip

00:38:08.900 --> 00:38:10.400
up his butt?

00:38:10.400 --> 00:38:14.360
Well, it is true
that the money wasn't

00:38:14.360 --> 00:38:16.550
very much in the scheme
of American budgets.

00:38:16.550 --> 00:38:20.780
It represented about
1/40 of 1% of one copper

00:38:20.780 --> 00:38:25.160
penny for every man, and woman,
and child in this democracy.

00:38:25.160 --> 00:38:27.590
Nevertheless, as was
frequently pointed out,

00:38:27.590 --> 00:38:30.230
it was public
money all the same,

00:38:30.230 --> 00:38:32.900
and the point made by Jesse
Helms and Donald Wildmon

00:38:32.900 --> 00:38:36.680
and their ilk was that
no sum, however small,

00:38:36.680 --> 00:38:40.100
ought to be spent by government
on the dissemination of images

00:38:40.100 --> 00:38:43.970
or ideas, that some part
of the taxpaying public

00:38:43.970 --> 00:38:46.702
might find morally repulsive.

00:38:46.702 --> 00:38:49.880
Therefore, let these
authors do their stuff,

00:38:49.880 --> 00:38:53.390
as the saying went, on their
own time and on their own dime,

00:38:53.390 --> 00:38:56.450
and never mind that neither
Mapplethorpe nor Serrano

00:38:56.450 --> 00:38:58.550
actually received a
cent of government money

00:38:58.550 --> 00:39:02.070
themselves from making
their disputed images.

00:39:02.070 --> 00:39:04.640
In other words, to qualify
for government support

00:39:04.640 --> 00:39:07.700
through the NEA,
a work of art had

00:39:07.700 --> 00:39:09.950
to satisfy what
these people imagine

00:39:09.950 --> 00:39:12.260
to be a moral consensus.

00:39:12.260 --> 00:39:17.040
It had to be, in essence,
incapable of offending anybody,

00:39:17.040 --> 00:39:19.820
no matter how low his or
her threshold of offense

00:39:19.820 --> 00:39:22.320
might actually be.

00:39:22.320 --> 00:39:26.060
Now, the obvious problem is
that in a society containing

00:39:26.060 --> 00:39:28.790
something like 260
million people,

00:39:28.790 --> 00:39:31.490
there is probably
no such work of art,

00:39:31.490 --> 00:39:34.040
not even a nude by
Renoir or a naked baby

00:39:34.040 --> 00:39:36.910
Jesus by Andrea Della Robbia.

00:39:36.910 --> 00:39:38.840
The insolubility of
the problem appears

00:39:38.840 --> 00:39:41.290
if you reflect upon
the amendments which

00:39:41.290 --> 00:39:43.660
Jesse Helms and Al
D'Amato and Orrin

00:39:43.660 --> 00:39:46.410
Hatch, those Three
Musketeers of Morality,

00:39:46.410 --> 00:39:50.310
tried to read into law.

00:39:50.310 --> 00:39:53.160
The Helms agreement proposed
that no government funds

00:39:53.160 --> 00:39:58.110
should be given by the NEA to
quote "promote, disseminate,

00:39:58.110 --> 00:40:01.410
or produce" in its
exact words, "one,

00:40:01.410 --> 00:40:04.050
obscene or indecent
materials, including

00:40:04.050 --> 00:40:07.470
but not limited to,
depictions of sado-masochism,

00:40:07.470 --> 00:40:10.620
homoeroticism, the
exploitation of children,

00:40:10.620 --> 00:40:13.530
or individuals
engaged in sex acts,

00:40:13.530 --> 00:40:17.640
or two, material which
denigrates the objects

00:40:17.640 --> 00:40:21.810
or beliefs of the adherents
of a particular religion

00:40:21.810 --> 00:40:30.280
or non-religion,
or three, material

00:40:30.280 --> 00:40:34.640
which denigrates, debases,
or reviles a person, group,

00:40:34.640 --> 00:40:39.190
or class of citizens on the
basis of race, creed, sex,

00:40:39.190 --> 00:40:42.190
handicap, age, or
national origin."

00:40:42.190 --> 00:40:44.860
Unquote.

00:40:44.860 --> 00:40:48.760
The most obvious and curious
feature of the Helms amendment

00:40:48.760 --> 00:40:53.200
was, if it had not issued from
the lips of a famously right

00:40:53.200 --> 00:40:56.530
wing Republican senator,
you could have mistaken it--

00:40:56.530 --> 00:40:59.380
and especially clauses
two and three--

00:40:59.380 --> 00:41:03.010
for any ruling on campus
speech limitations recently

00:41:03.010 --> 00:41:05.740
proposed by those other
self-appointed censors who

00:41:05.740 --> 00:41:08.200
are cutting loose
in harried society,

00:41:08.200 --> 00:41:09.890
those hypocritical
pests of academia

00:41:09.890 --> 00:41:12.610
and the politically correct.

00:41:12.610 --> 00:41:16.870
It was hard to know
exactly what Helms

00:41:16.870 --> 00:41:21.529
meant by a particular
religion or non-religion.

00:41:21.529 --> 00:41:24.980
[LAUGHING]

00:41:24.980 --> 00:41:25.640
Pantheism.

00:41:29.570 --> 00:41:31.940
But certainly,
clause three made it

00:41:31.940 --> 00:41:34.970
clear that he was against
racism, sexism, ablism,

00:41:34.970 --> 00:41:37.670
lookism, and any of
the other offenses

00:41:37.670 --> 00:41:40.790
against social etiquette,
whose proscription by PC

00:41:40.790 --> 00:41:43.940
was already causing such
mirth and laughter among older

00:41:43.940 --> 00:41:46.400
conservatives
off-campus and creating

00:41:46.400 --> 00:41:48.830
not a few new ones on it.

00:41:48.830 --> 00:41:51.470
And thus, extremes tend to meet.

00:41:51.470 --> 00:41:54.590
In fact, the Helms
agreement was so broadly

00:41:54.590 --> 00:41:56.750
drafted as to be
virtually meaningless.

00:41:56.750 --> 00:41:58.670
Its effect would
have been to create

00:41:58.670 --> 00:42:01.850
a kind of loony parody
of cultural democracy

00:42:01.850 --> 00:42:06.740
in which every citizen became
his or her own Cato the Censor.

00:42:06.740 --> 00:42:09.500
All the work of art would
need to be defunded,

00:42:09.500 --> 00:42:12.500
or if shown in a museum
under NEA auspices,

00:42:12.500 --> 00:42:16.670
removed from view, would be to
offend anybody for practically

00:42:16.670 --> 00:42:18.050
any reason at all.

00:42:18.050 --> 00:42:20.480
The amendment was thus a
sort of crystallization

00:42:20.480 --> 00:42:24.862
in very pure form of our present
culture of complaint into law.

00:42:24.862 --> 00:42:26.570
It would have made
the National Endowment

00:42:26.570 --> 00:42:29.990
hostage to every crank,
ideologue, and God botherer

00:42:29.990 --> 00:42:31.700
in America.

00:42:31.700 --> 00:42:35.114
A grounds for the exhibition
of, say, Gothic Ivories

00:42:35.114 --> 00:42:37.280
could be pulled on the
grounds that the material was

00:42:37.280 --> 00:42:40.490
objectionable to Jews,
since much medieval art is

00:42:40.490 --> 00:42:44.870
deeply anti-Semitic, or to
Muslims, because who know?

00:42:44.870 --> 00:42:47.900
One of those ivories might have
contained a not uncommon figure

00:42:47.900 --> 00:42:50.900
of false prophets boiling
in hell with Muhammad, which

00:42:50.900 --> 00:42:53.450
was quite a favorite
in the Middle Ages,

00:42:53.450 --> 00:42:55.430
or for that matter,
to atheists offended

00:42:55.430 --> 00:42:57.500
by the use of government
money to insert

00:42:57.500 --> 00:42:59.570
any sort of
religious propaganda,

00:42:59.570 --> 00:43:03.020
including ancient ivory
carvings into a museum.

00:43:03.020 --> 00:43:06.290
And indeed, one could imagine
some Stalinist feminist

00:43:06.290 --> 00:43:13.800
complaining that a 13th century
depiction of a patriarchal god,

00:43:13.800 --> 00:43:18.110
or the sexism implicit in a
subservient or tempting Eve,

00:43:18.110 --> 00:43:21.266
was repugnant to her
religion or non-religion.

00:43:21.266 --> 00:43:23.600
[APPLAUSE]

00:43:23.600 --> 00:43:26.240
Under the Helms amendment,
a fire worshipper

00:43:26.240 --> 00:43:28.640
might well claim that the
presence of extinguishers

00:43:28.640 --> 00:43:31.372
in the museum was
repulsive to his deity.

00:43:31.372 --> 00:43:33.035
[LAUGHING]

00:43:33.035 --> 00:43:34.910
Now Helms and his
supporters were, of course,

00:43:34.910 --> 00:43:37.100
at pains to deny that the
amendment had anything

00:43:37.100 --> 00:43:39.080
at all to do with censorship.

00:43:39.080 --> 00:43:40.760
Nevertheless, one
would have needed

00:43:40.760 --> 00:43:44.630
to be remarkably naive to
suppose that censorship was not

00:43:44.630 --> 00:43:46.970
the root of this controversy.

00:43:46.970 --> 00:43:48.950
The efforts to cut
off government money

00:43:48.950 --> 00:43:53.090
from offensive art were but the
tip of a general effort growing

00:43:53.090 --> 00:43:56.760
on the right to repress
all offensive art,

00:43:56.760 --> 00:44:00.300
subsidized or not, under
the assumption basically

00:44:00.300 --> 00:44:04.130
of their proponents that the
stuff isn't a necessary part

00:44:04.130 --> 00:44:06.050
of social discourse anyway.

00:44:06.050 --> 00:44:07.790
The wisest analysis
of this problem

00:44:07.790 --> 00:44:10.760
was offered some months
later by a Jesuit,

00:44:10.760 --> 00:44:13.490
rather the Reverend Timothy
Healy, the Director of the New

00:44:13.490 --> 00:44:17.040
York Public Library,
now alas dead.

00:44:17.040 --> 00:44:19.140
The debate is about
censorship, Father Healy

00:44:19.140 --> 00:44:22.080
declared to a house subcommittee
post-secondary education

00:44:22.080 --> 00:44:24.530
in November 1989.

00:44:24.530 --> 00:44:26.640
And he went on,
"Against the argument

00:44:26.640 --> 00:44:29.340
that the artist was free to
write, to paint, or to compose

00:44:29.340 --> 00:44:31.410
as he pleases without
federal subsidy,

00:44:31.410 --> 00:44:35.280
we must urge that to deprive an
artist of that subsidy because

00:44:35.280 --> 00:44:38.730
of the content of his work
is a clear and strong kind

00:44:38.730 --> 00:44:40.180
of censorship.

00:44:40.180 --> 00:44:42.810
The counter-argument
is really a dodge.

00:44:42.810 --> 00:44:45.840
The mixing up of the realm of
law with the realm of morals

00:44:45.840 --> 00:44:47.280
is deadly.

00:44:47.280 --> 00:44:50.850
Law can tolerate evils
that morality condemns.

00:44:50.850 --> 00:44:55.460
We have a good law if it can be
obeyed, if it is enforceable,

00:44:55.460 --> 00:44:57.680
and if it is so
prudently drafted

00:44:57.680 --> 00:44:59.820
that it avoids most of
the harmful effects that

00:44:59.820 --> 00:45:01.530
flow from it.

00:45:01.530 --> 00:45:03.900
If a law does none
of these things,

00:45:03.900 --> 00:45:07.050
it is a bad law, no
matter what the logic

00:45:07.050 --> 00:45:09.280
or the moral
intensity behind it."

00:45:09.280 --> 00:45:10.864
Unquote.

00:45:10.864 --> 00:45:12.280
Though none of the
arguments heard

00:45:12.280 --> 00:45:15.520
on the Senate floor attained
the clarity and sophistication

00:45:15.520 --> 00:45:19.390
of this priest's words, the
Helms amendment was voted down.

00:45:19.390 --> 00:45:21.850
But its defeat did
not dispel the idea

00:45:21.850 --> 00:45:24.040
that the US
government implicitly

00:45:24.040 --> 00:45:27.100
puts its moral weight
behind all the art

00:45:27.100 --> 00:45:31.610
that it funds, that the artist
with an NEA grant becomes,

00:45:31.610 --> 00:45:34.420
as it were, the
voice of the state,

00:45:34.420 --> 00:45:38.050
and that the state is therefore
to be held responsible for what

00:45:38.050 --> 00:45:42.360
he or she makes or says.

00:45:42.360 --> 00:45:44.670
The NEA, however, it
was never conceived

00:45:44.670 --> 00:45:47.610
as an arm of state propaganda,
except in the very general

00:45:47.610 --> 00:45:50.640
sense that Kennedy,
Johnson, and their advisors

00:45:50.640 --> 00:45:52.980
felt that America should
support its own artists

00:45:52.980 --> 00:45:56.680
and would, in some general
way, look better for doing so.

00:45:56.680 --> 00:46:01.090
There is a huge difference
between commissioning a work

00:46:01.090 --> 00:46:05.380
of art and sponsoring a talent.

00:46:05.380 --> 00:46:09.290
The patron says, when he
is commissioning something,

00:46:09.290 --> 00:46:12.290
I want a mural with me on
horseback showing democracy

00:46:12.290 --> 00:46:13.430
trampling on ignorance.

00:46:13.430 --> 00:46:17.320
And moreover after that,
I want to own the mural.

00:46:17.320 --> 00:46:23.380
The NEA essentially puts a
bet on talent at arm's length

00:46:23.380 --> 00:46:26.500
through the peer review
system, and then it

00:46:26.500 --> 00:46:28.430
gets out of the way.

00:46:28.430 --> 00:46:32.740
Once you forget the difference
between state commissioning

00:46:32.740 --> 00:46:37.360
and this kind of sponsorship,
every attack upon the NEA

00:46:37.360 --> 00:46:38.200
becomes possible.

00:46:38.200 --> 00:46:41.530
I mean, this was the confusion
behind the culture war's

00:46:41.530 --> 00:46:43.870
equivalent of the
Willie Horton ad.

00:46:43.870 --> 00:46:47.890
Pat Buchanan was noted a TV
spot attacking George Bush

00:46:47.890 --> 00:46:50.800
through the NEA and PBS.

00:46:50.800 --> 00:46:52.960
Many of you must have seen
this when you saw clips

00:46:52.960 --> 00:46:56.920
from a documentary that had
been made for PBS about gays.

00:46:56.920 --> 00:46:59.170
The clips with leather queens,
capering in the street,

00:46:59.170 --> 00:47:00.720
and of course, of the March.

00:47:00.720 --> 00:47:02.575
And over this
appalling Saturnalia,

00:47:02.575 --> 00:47:04.570
their came the voice
of a narrator saying

00:47:04.570 --> 00:47:07.450
that this is what George
Bush was investing your tax

00:47:07.450 --> 00:47:09.110
dollars in.

00:47:09.110 --> 00:47:11.180
Note the use of the verb.

00:47:11.180 --> 00:47:14.630
Investing in half-naked
gays, the way

00:47:14.630 --> 00:47:17.680
that his father used
to invest in oil money.

00:47:17.680 --> 00:47:19.910
Here was Bush putting
your money into a kind

00:47:19.910 --> 00:47:23.120
of wildcat scheme for the
propagation of homosexuality.

00:47:25.850 --> 00:47:27.890
But actually, that
isn't the way it works.

00:47:27.890 --> 00:47:32.240
The idea behind the
NEA fundamentally

00:47:32.240 --> 00:47:37.790
is that a democracy can
subsidize a forum, an agora,

00:47:37.790 --> 00:47:39.890
a marketplace of
ideas and images,

00:47:39.890 --> 00:47:44.120
without specifying in advance
what kind of images and ideas

00:47:44.120 --> 00:47:46.980
should be displayed in it.

00:47:46.980 --> 00:47:50.877
This certainly relies upon
some kind of an agreement.

00:47:50.877 --> 00:47:52.710
Some kind of an agreement
of common interest

00:47:52.710 --> 00:47:56.150
between the sponsor
and the artist.

00:47:56.150 --> 00:47:57.900
But there are plenty
of precedents for it,

00:47:57.900 --> 00:47:59.640
and there are plenty
of working parallels

00:47:59.640 --> 00:48:02.580
to it in American life already.

00:48:02.580 --> 00:48:05.170
When, for instance, a
university gets federal, state,

00:48:05.170 --> 00:48:08.370
or city funds, they
come with the assumption

00:48:08.370 --> 00:48:11.730
that the place will remain
free from ideological pressure,

00:48:11.730 --> 00:48:14.820
and that any issue can be
reasonably exposed and debated

00:48:14.820 --> 00:48:16.230
on campus.

00:48:16.230 --> 00:48:21.130
If this isn't so, the
university, state-subsidized,

00:48:21.130 --> 00:48:24.570
wholly or partially, cannot
function as a place of teaching

00:48:24.570 --> 00:48:25.560
and learning.

00:48:25.560 --> 00:48:28.170
The fact that public
money is involved

00:48:28.170 --> 00:48:30.780
does not mean that the
university should avoid,

00:48:30.780 --> 00:48:33.690
or skirt controversial
ideas, or punish people

00:48:33.690 --> 00:48:35.500
for uttering them.

00:48:35.500 --> 00:48:38.740
The university has
to stick to that,

00:48:38.740 --> 00:48:40.600
no matter how
inconvenient it may be,

00:48:40.600 --> 00:48:42.640
and it sometimes is
extremely inconvenient.

00:48:42.640 --> 00:48:44.920
For example, I and
many other people

00:48:44.920 --> 00:48:48.130
regard Leonard Jeffries
as an anti-Semitic madman,

00:48:48.130 --> 00:48:50.460
whose scholarship is farcical.

00:48:50.460 --> 00:48:53.230
But you cannot get rid of him
now by saying that the views

00:48:53.230 --> 00:48:55.990
that he teaches are
offensive to most Americans,

00:48:55.990 --> 00:48:59.530
blacks included, and that
since CCNY gets dollars from

00:48:59.530 --> 00:49:03.460
the taxpayer, he should for
that reason be forced out.

00:49:03.460 --> 00:49:05.770
On an even more basic
level, the city,

00:49:05.770 --> 00:49:08.980
like any other in America, has
parks and squares and streets

00:49:08.980 --> 00:49:11.680
which are maintained
by public funds.

00:49:11.680 --> 00:49:14.860
And yet, it is perfectly legal
to express your views in them,

00:49:14.860 --> 00:49:17.500
however repugnant those
views may be to others.

00:49:17.500 --> 00:49:19.810
That is a basic part
of citizens' rights

00:49:19.810 --> 00:49:21.130
under the First Amendment.

00:49:21.130 --> 00:49:24.460
If I want to demonstrate
for or against abortion

00:49:24.460 --> 00:49:27.880
in that state-sponsored
civic space, I can.

00:49:27.880 --> 00:49:30.880
If I want to propose
voting rights for insects

00:49:30.880 --> 00:49:34.330
or urge the immediate
repatriation of all Catholics

00:49:34.330 --> 00:49:36.460
from Boston to Ireland,
I can do that too.

00:49:36.460 --> 00:49:38.770
I may not succeed,
but I can do it.

00:49:38.770 --> 00:49:41.230
The assumption behind
this and behind the NEA

00:49:41.230 --> 00:49:46.090
too is that the diversity
in a democracy is good.

00:49:46.090 --> 00:49:49.240
We agree to pool our
tax dollars to create

00:49:49.240 --> 00:49:53.680
a general frame in which
opinions can be freely uttered

00:49:53.680 --> 00:49:56.170
and images freely propagated.

00:49:56.170 --> 00:49:59.830
Any opinions, however much
they may annoy or offend

00:49:59.830 --> 00:50:02.950
somebody else who
also has the right

00:50:02.950 --> 00:50:06.280
to express his or her
opinions, no matter

00:50:06.280 --> 00:50:08.800
repugnant I might find them.

00:50:08.800 --> 00:50:12.130
Does this mean that the
NEA is obliged to subsidize

00:50:12.130 --> 00:50:14.040
any and every kind
of utterance without

00:50:14.040 --> 00:50:16.420
a static moral, or political,
or other distinctions,

00:50:16.420 --> 00:50:17.920
or considerations?

00:50:17.920 --> 00:50:19.390
I don't believe so.

00:50:19.390 --> 00:50:22.230
No sponsor, still less
patron, in the history of art

00:50:22.230 --> 00:50:24.490
has ever done this.

00:50:24.490 --> 00:50:26.850
But it does mean
that the NEA has

00:50:26.850 --> 00:50:29.670
to be prepared to resist
unreasonable pressure,

00:50:29.670 --> 00:50:32.460
not only from the
moralists and censors

00:50:32.460 --> 00:50:35.310
of the fundamentalist right,
with their cant about family

00:50:35.310 --> 00:50:38.760
values, but also from
the censorious left,

00:50:38.760 --> 00:50:41.440
with its consistent betrayal
of the liberal ideal

00:50:41.440 --> 00:50:44.430
that high cultural
standards can be popularly

00:50:44.430 --> 00:50:46.740
accepted and practiced.

00:50:46.740 --> 00:50:49.560
It has got to shift
its attention away

00:50:49.560 --> 00:50:53.370
from the cliches of our time,
the chatter about empowerment,

00:50:53.370 --> 00:50:57.030
the obsession with resentment
as a cultural standard.

00:50:57.030 --> 00:51:00.360
There is a right tool of
device, and there is a left one,

00:51:00.360 --> 00:51:03.600
and between the two, the
NEA is painfully squeezed

00:51:03.600 --> 00:51:07.280
and may, in the end, be
distorted out of useful shape.

00:51:07.280 --> 00:51:09.650
These pressures
are or the outcome

00:51:09.650 --> 00:51:13.340
both of the PC belief that the
personal is the political, acts

00:51:13.340 --> 00:51:17.000
of imagination not exempted,
and of the conservative view,

00:51:17.000 --> 00:51:19.930
that any stick you can beat
liberals with is a good stick,

00:51:19.930 --> 00:51:23.312
and never mind what else gets
flattened in the struggle.

00:51:23.312 --> 00:51:24.770
The National
Endowment for the Arts

00:51:24.770 --> 00:51:28.220
was not, in fact, designed to
be an arena for such disputes,

00:51:28.220 --> 00:51:30.560
and thus it proved
extraordinarily awkward

00:51:30.560 --> 00:51:32.570
and even inept at
responding to them.

00:51:32.570 --> 00:51:37.850
And its response is complicated
by the claims of activist art

00:51:37.850 --> 00:51:40.700
to constitute an avant garde.

00:51:40.700 --> 00:51:43.400
The population of the art
world expanded enormously

00:51:43.400 --> 00:51:45.240
in the 1980s.

00:51:45.240 --> 00:51:47.930
And in an overpopulated art
world with a depressed market,

00:51:47.930 --> 00:51:50.810
you are going to hear more
and more about discrimination

00:51:50.810 --> 00:51:53.490
against artists, endless
complaints about racism,

00:51:53.490 --> 00:51:58.190
sexism, and so on, whereas the
fundamental underlying problem

00:51:58.190 --> 00:52:00.950
is that there are too many
artists of all kinds in America

00:52:00.950 --> 00:52:05.410
for the base of collectors
and sponsors to support.

00:52:05.410 --> 00:52:07.720
That is why the National
Endowment for the Arts really

00:52:07.720 --> 00:52:10.630
matters in the ecology
of American culture,

00:52:10.630 --> 00:52:12.970
but it cannot do good
work, in my opinion,

00:52:12.970 --> 00:52:17.290
if it caves in to the forced
politics of group entitlement.

00:52:17.290 --> 00:52:20.170
The sense of disenfranchisement
among artists

00:52:20.170 --> 00:52:23.620
has led to a stream of attacks
upon the idea of quality,

00:52:23.620 --> 00:52:25.390
as though it were
the enemy of justice.

00:52:25.390 --> 00:52:27.940
Quality, the argument
goes, is a plot.

00:52:27.940 --> 00:52:30.640
It is the result of a
conspiracy to marginalize

00:52:30.640 --> 00:52:33.190
the work of other
races, other cultures,

00:52:33.190 --> 00:52:37.720
by the patriarchal penis people.

00:52:37.720 --> 00:52:39.670
And to invoke its
presence in works of art

00:52:39.670 --> 00:52:42.190
is somehow inherently
repressive.

00:52:42.190 --> 00:52:44.920
This drivel has become
the new orthodoxy,

00:52:44.920 --> 00:52:47.470
and fewer and fewer
critics and historians

00:52:47.470 --> 00:52:49.360
seem able to resist it.

00:52:49.360 --> 00:52:52.540
More and more, it is assumed
that one's cultural reach

00:52:52.540 --> 00:52:55.510
is fixed and determined
forever by whatever

00:52:55.510 --> 00:52:57.606
slot one is raised in.

00:52:57.606 --> 00:52:59.230
You can imagine the
contempt with which

00:52:59.230 --> 00:53:02.020
a great Mexican artist
like Diego Rivera

00:53:02.020 --> 00:53:03.610
would have reacted to this.

00:53:03.610 --> 00:53:05.500
Rivera probably
gave more to Mexico

00:53:05.500 --> 00:53:08.170
in terms of self-knowledge
and cultural pride

00:53:08.170 --> 00:53:11.260
than any artist whose name we
know in its entire history,

00:53:11.260 --> 00:53:14.200
but he was only able to do
so because he had absorbed

00:53:14.200 --> 00:53:17.050
and completely internalized the
great tradition of Renaissance

00:53:17.050 --> 00:53:19.900
Fresco painting, which
combined with his absorption

00:53:19.900 --> 00:53:23.020
in French modernism,
pre-Colombian Mexican art,

00:53:23.020 --> 00:53:26.800
and living folk art to produce
the tremendous results that we

00:53:26.800 --> 00:53:30.850
see on such walls as those
of Palacio Nacional in Mexico

00:53:30.850 --> 00:53:31.930
City.

00:53:31.930 --> 00:53:35.030
If you had told Rivera
that quality didn't matter,

00:53:35.030 --> 00:53:37.330
he would have
laughed in your face.

00:53:37.330 --> 00:53:41.170
Now you can't deny that at any
time in the history of art,

00:53:41.170 --> 00:53:43.350
there have been
inflated reputations

00:53:43.350 --> 00:53:44.860
and wrongly ignored artists.

00:53:44.860 --> 00:53:48.190
Certainly, this is as
true today as it ever was.

00:53:48.190 --> 00:53:50.890
This happens in the short-term,
but in the long run,

00:53:50.890 --> 00:53:53.140
the injustices do
tend to be corrected.

00:53:53.140 --> 00:53:55.780
There remains, for
instance, practically

00:53:55.780 --> 00:53:58.900
no institutional bias against
women artists in America

00:53:58.900 --> 00:54:01.270
any more than there is
a bias against women

00:54:01.270 --> 00:54:03.340
writers in American publishing.

00:54:03.340 --> 00:54:05.650
And this is certainly very
different to the America

00:54:05.650 --> 00:54:08.260
that I first came to in 1970.

00:54:08.260 --> 00:54:12.350
And yet, you have to bear in
mind that not all artists are

00:54:12.350 --> 00:54:13.835
wrongly ignored.

00:54:13.835 --> 00:54:17.480
[LAUGHING]

00:54:17.480 --> 00:54:22.160
Mediocrity is the natural fate
of most creative endeavor,

00:54:22.160 --> 00:54:25.430
especially in a society, which
in the name of therapeutics

00:54:25.430 --> 00:54:28.490
and personal growth, has
removed most of the rigor

00:54:28.490 --> 00:54:30.676
from the training
of its artists.

00:54:30.676 --> 00:54:33.532
[APPLAUSE]

00:54:36.400 --> 00:54:40.210
Most art made by white
Americans is mediocre.

00:54:40.210 --> 00:54:43.830
Most made by black
Americans is mediocre.

00:54:43.830 --> 00:54:47.200
Most art made by Chinese
Americans, Japanese Americans,

00:54:47.200 --> 00:54:49.900
and American Indians
is mediocre too.

00:54:49.900 --> 00:54:52.570
And most art made by
women is as mediocre

00:54:52.570 --> 00:54:55.000
as most art made by men.

00:54:55.000 --> 00:54:57.130
The same applies to
gays and straights

00:54:57.130 --> 00:54:59.980
and to all the other polarities.

00:54:59.980 --> 00:55:04.630
We must therefore be extremely
wary of the sentimentality

00:55:04.630 --> 00:55:08.710
that institutional
multi-culti breeds.

00:55:08.710 --> 00:55:11.200
Those who talk about
remedial multiculturalism

00:55:11.200 --> 00:55:13.360
as a radical program--

00:55:13.360 --> 00:55:15.790
and as anybody who has
read my book will know,

00:55:15.790 --> 00:55:18.310
I am very much a friend
of multiculturalism,

00:55:18.310 --> 00:55:20.380
and I am totally in favor of it.

00:55:20.380 --> 00:55:23.410
Otherwise, I would never
have left my own country

00:55:23.410 --> 00:55:26.890
to begin with in
order to start trying

00:55:26.890 --> 00:55:30.640
to see through the
scrims that divide us

00:55:30.640 --> 00:55:32.440
and understand other
peoples image banks.

00:55:32.440 --> 00:55:37.150
But this is not what is meant
so often by multiculturalism

00:55:37.150 --> 00:55:37.970
in this society.

00:55:37.970 --> 00:55:40.710
What is meant so often
is a sort of separatism.

00:55:40.710 --> 00:55:43.300
Anyway, those who talk
about remedial multi-culti

00:55:43.300 --> 00:55:46.830
as a radical program fail
to see how conventionalizing

00:55:46.830 --> 00:55:49.980
and soothing and altogether
consoling to middlebrow

00:55:49.980 --> 00:55:52.150
taste its effects
can tend to be.

00:55:52.150 --> 00:55:55.870
One already sees this
in one area, that

00:55:55.870 --> 00:55:58.090
of publicly funded artware.

00:55:58.090 --> 00:56:01.840
Multicultural programs serve
to get the embattled National

00:56:01.840 --> 00:56:04.180
Endowment for the Arts
off the hook of making

00:56:04.180 --> 00:56:06.860
any discriminations at all.

00:56:06.860 --> 00:56:09.530
If the Philistines
are begging for an end

00:56:09.530 --> 00:56:12.210
to government
support for high art,

00:56:12.210 --> 00:56:15.160
for difficult and possibly
controversial art,

00:56:15.160 --> 00:56:17.210
and if meanwhile the
moralists are yelling

00:56:17.210 --> 00:56:20.060
for the blood of any poet or
performer whose work might

00:56:20.060 --> 00:56:23.480
raise hackles in some
golf club in Tulsa,

00:56:23.480 --> 00:56:27.800
then what could be a better
refuge than multiculturalism.

00:56:27.800 --> 00:56:31.760
Just turn the already meager
trickle of government money

00:56:31.760 --> 00:56:34.220
towards stuff that nobody
could quarrel with,

00:56:34.220 --> 00:56:37.280
areas such as-- to point
from a recent brochure,

00:56:37.280 --> 00:56:40.610
issued by the NEA's last
director, Anne-Imelda Radice--

00:56:40.610 --> 00:56:46.730
quote, "Hmong needlework,
coastal sea grass basketry,

00:56:46.730 --> 00:56:50.990
Southeast Alaskan native
dance, American Indian basketry

00:56:50.990 --> 00:56:54.020
and woodcraft, Pacific
island canoe building,

00:56:54.020 --> 00:56:57.240
and Appalachian banjo playing."

00:56:57.240 --> 00:57:00.050
this sentimental form
of cultural diversity as

00:57:00.050 --> 00:57:03.410
interpreted by federal funding
agencies and an increasing

00:57:03.410 --> 00:57:06.950
number of private
foundations may or may not

00:57:06.950 --> 00:57:09.290
pick some good stuff
out of the woodpile.

00:57:09.290 --> 00:57:12.860
But most of the time,
entails pious gestures

00:57:12.860 --> 00:57:15.920
of cultural support while
actually producing remarkably

00:57:15.920 --> 00:57:18.500
little that would,
in aesthetic terms,

00:57:18.500 --> 00:57:21.650
challenge, refine,
criticize, or in any way

00:57:21.650 --> 00:57:24.440
extend the thinking
of the status quo.

00:57:24.440 --> 00:57:27.590
Under the rubric of
self-esteem, they

00:57:27.590 --> 00:57:31.790
foster mediocre for a populist
mentality that contents itself

00:57:31.790 --> 00:57:34.400
with relatively
easy tasks expressed

00:57:34.400 --> 00:57:36.980
in the jargon of supporting
ethnicity and gender

00:57:36.980 --> 00:57:40.580
differences in the arts, instead
of the harder one of looking

00:57:40.580 --> 00:57:42.770
for real excellence.

00:57:42.770 --> 00:57:46.640
Too much of the art that
results from such programs

00:57:46.640 --> 00:57:49.520
turns out to be
affirmative prolix kitsch,

00:57:49.520 --> 00:57:52.310
gushing with sincerity,
and people like it

00:57:52.310 --> 00:57:53.900
for the same reasons
that they like

00:57:53.900 --> 00:57:56.350
Hallmark Cards
with sumi-e herons

00:57:56.350 --> 00:57:57.920
and New Age verses on them.

00:57:57.920 --> 00:58:00.250
It just makes them feel good.

00:58:00.250 --> 00:58:02.450
[LAUGHING]

00:58:02.450 --> 00:58:07.010
Now you can certainly run a
feel-good cultural program

00:58:07.010 --> 00:58:09.740
on sociological and
statistical criteria,

00:58:09.740 --> 00:58:12.800
backing it up with the usual
pieties about empowerment.

00:58:12.800 --> 00:58:16.880
And this conforms very well
to the evangelical tradition

00:58:16.880 --> 00:58:20.090
of American cultural life,
the idea that one is morally

00:58:20.090 --> 00:58:23.360
improved, uplifted, and
turned into a better citizen

00:58:23.360 --> 00:58:25.970
by producing or consuming art.

00:58:25.970 --> 00:58:29.210
But the art that it fosters
maybe, and sometimes

00:58:29.210 --> 00:58:31.144
is, quite banal.

00:58:31.144 --> 00:58:32.560
What happens where
government arts

00:58:32.560 --> 00:58:36.230
money and populist
multiculturalism intersect?

00:58:36.230 --> 00:58:38.270
All too often a form
of moral blackmail,

00:58:38.270 --> 00:58:40.520
with one eye on the pork barrel.

00:58:40.520 --> 00:58:43.430
Now in Holland for
the last 20 years,

00:58:43.430 --> 00:58:47.000
they tried a form of popular
support for everybody.

00:58:47.000 --> 00:58:51.920
The government set up a fund to
buy work by artists no matter

00:58:51.920 --> 00:58:53.897
how good it was.

00:58:53.897 --> 00:58:55.730
All that mattered was
that the artist should

00:58:55.730 --> 00:58:59.690
be A, alive, and B, Dutch.

00:58:59.690 --> 00:59:04.640
About 8,000 Dutch artists are
represented in that collection.

00:59:04.640 --> 00:59:06.490
None of it is shown.

00:59:06.490 --> 00:59:08.720
And as everybody in
Holland, including

00:59:08.720 --> 00:59:12.800
most of the artists involved, I
feel now admits about 98% of it

00:59:12.800 --> 00:59:14.000
is rubbish.

00:59:14.000 --> 00:59:16.130
The storage expenses
are so high that they

00:59:16.130 --> 00:59:17.900
have to get rid of the stuff.

00:59:17.900 --> 00:59:21.260
But they can't because
nobody wants it.

00:59:21.260 --> 00:59:23.360
They tried giving it
to public institutions,

00:59:23.360 --> 00:59:25.745
like lunatic asylum
and hospitals.

00:59:25.745 --> 00:59:27.230
[LAUGHING]

00:59:27.230 --> 00:59:31.670
But even the lunatic asylums
insisted upon standards.

00:59:31.670 --> 00:59:34.650
They wanted to pick and choose.

00:59:34.650 --> 00:59:37.580
So there it all sits.

00:59:37.580 --> 00:59:42.470
Democratic, non-hierarchical,
non-elitist, nonsexist,

00:59:42.470 --> 00:59:44.390
unsalable, and to
the great regret

00:59:44.390 --> 00:59:48.228
of the Dutch authorities,
only partially biodegradable.

00:59:48.228 --> 00:59:50.970
[LAUGHING]

00:59:50.970 --> 00:59:53.710
Now there are lessons from this,
and let me suggest two of them.

00:59:53.710 --> 00:59:56.280
The first is that if Dutch
madhouses can discriminate

00:59:56.280 --> 01:00:00.030
about art without being accused
of anti-democratic elitism,

01:00:00.030 --> 01:00:02.370
so can the NEA.

01:00:02.370 --> 01:00:05.830
But mind you, I speak as
an unrepentant elitist.

01:00:05.830 --> 01:00:09.210
I don't think democracy
is any guide to taste.

01:00:09.210 --> 01:00:11.850
I think the job of democracy
in the field of art

01:00:11.850 --> 01:00:14.368
is to make the world
safe for elitism.

01:00:14.368 --> 01:00:18.192
[LAUGHING]

01:00:18.192 --> 01:00:22.020
[APPLAUSE]

01:00:22.020 --> 01:00:26.280
Not an elitism based upon race,
or money, or social position,

01:00:26.280 --> 01:00:28.210
but on skill and imagination.

01:00:28.210 --> 01:00:30.610
And the embodiment of high
ability and intense vision

01:00:30.610 --> 01:00:34.060
is basically the only thing
that does make art popular.

01:00:34.060 --> 01:00:36.460
It is why the Rijksmuseum
is full of people

01:00:36.460 --> 01:00:40.120
and the remedial art basements
of Amsterdam are not.

01:00:40.120 --> 01:00:42.910
The greatest popular
spectacles in America elitist

01:00:42.910 --> 01:00:43.960
to the core--

01:00:43.960 --> 01:00:48.860
football games, basketball
games, professional tennis.

01:00:48.860 --> 01:00:51.680
But nobody is going to
pay to watch Hilton Kramer

01:00:51.680 --> 01:00:54.830
and me swim the 800 meter
freestyle in 35 minutes

01:00:54.830 --> 01:00:57.650
flat despite our relatively
privileged position

01:00:57.650 --> 01:00:59.970
as white European males.

01:00:59.970 --> 01:01:02.660
Like sport, art is
an area in which

01:01:02.660 --> 01:01:07.940
elitism can display itself at a
negligible cost in social harm.

01:01:07.940 --> 01:01:11.180
The second lesson is that if
a scrupulous participatory

01:01:11.180 --> 01:01:15.530
democracy like Holland spends 25
times per taxpayer what America

01:01:15.530 --> 01:01:18.890
does on culture, choosing
art on sociological grounds

01:01:18.890 --> 01:01:21.140
in the name of complete
cultural egalitarian

01:01:21.140 --> 01:01:24.200
and then ends up with a
garbage disposal problem,

01:01:24.200 --> 01:01:27.500
what guarantee is there that
we can do much better here?

01:01:27.500 --> 01:01:30.950
Absolutely no guarantee
that I can see.

01:01:30.950 --> 01:01:33.980
It is in the nature of human
beings to discriminate.

01:01:33.980 --> 01:01:37.400
We make choices and
judgments every day.

01:01:37.400 --> 01:01:39.592
These choices are part
of real experience.

01:01:39.592 --> 01:01:41.300
They are influenced
by others, of course,

01:01:41.300 --> 01:01:43.550
but they are not
fundamentally the result

01:01:43.550 --> 01:01:45.410
of a passive reaction
to authority.

01:01:45.410 --> 01:01:49.190
And we know, I think, that
one of the realist experiences

01:01:49.190 --> 01:01:52.580
in cultural life is that
of a certain inequality

01:01:52.580 --> 01:01:55.460
between books, and musical
performances, and paintings,

01:01:55.460 --> 01:01:57.380
and other works of art.

01:01:57.380 --> 01:02:00.790
Some things do strike us
as better than others.

01:02:00.790 --> 01:02:01.880
Aesthetically better.

01:02:01.880 --> 01:02:03.050
More articulate.

01:02:03.050 --> 01:02:05.210
More radiant with consciousness.

01:02:05.210 --> 01:02:07.460
We may have
difficulty saying why,

01:02:07.460 --> 01:02:09.090
but the experience remains.

01:02:09.090 --> 01:02:12.200
An aesthetic
discrimination must not

01:02:12.200 --> 01:02:14.960
be tarred with the brush
of racial or gender

01:02:14.960 --> 01:02:16.730
discrimination.

01:02:16.730 --> 01:02:18.500
It corresponds to
experience, just

01:02:18.500 --> 01:02:21.380
as our perception and
comparison of grace

01:02:21.380 --> 01:02:24.320
in the work of a basketball
player or a tennis pro

01:02:24.320 --> 01:02:25.610
rises from experience.

01:02:25.610 --> 01:02:29.480
And these differences of
intensity and meaning and grace

01:02:29.480 --> 01:02:32.780
can't be set forth in a little
catechism or a recipe book.

01:02:32.780 --> 01:02:35.840
They can only be experienced,
and argued, and then

01:02:35.840 --> 01:02:38.540
seen in relation to a
history that, of course, must

01:02:38.540 --> 01:02:40.850
include social history.

01:02:40.850 --> 01:02:44.300
Now the NEA, as I understand
it, was founded in order

01:02:44.300 --> 01:02:48.980
to identify, to pick
out, those sparks

01:02:48.980 --> 01:02:51.440
in the cultural campfire
around which we all

01:02:51.440 --> 01:02:55.880
sit, wherever they may
be found, to fan them,

01:02:55.880 --> 01:02:59.750
to give them oxygen, to
bring them into flame.

01:02:59.750 --> 01:03:02.260
This is the way that sponsorship
makes its modest bets

01:03:02.260 --> 01:03:03.730
about the future.

01:03:03.730 --> 01:03:06.130
The moment you go
to other criteria,

01:03:06.130 --> 01:03:07.900
you may be in trouble.

01:03:07.900 --> 01:03:10.150
If you reform your
program in terms

01:03:10.150 --> 01:03:13.840
of agendas based on the artist's
race or gender or supposed

01:03:13.840 --> 01:03:17.050
degree of cultural
enfranchisement, if you start

01:03:17.050 --> 01:03:20.560
agonizing, for instance, about
whether the quota of grants

01:03:20.560 --> 01:03:24.310
to Hispanic activist video
artists has being fulfilled,

01:03:24.310 --> 01:03:26.710
you will have no answer
to the conservatives who

01:03:26.710 --> 01:03:29.530
then turn around and want to
know why the NEA isn't giving

01:03:29.530 --> 01:03:31.750
equal grants to Christian
fundamentalist poster

01:03:31.750 --> 01:03:34.000
designers.

01:03:34.000 --> 01:03:36.400
I am certainly an
elitist in that

01:03:36.400 --> 01:03:38.210
I believe in high
aesthetic standards,

01:03:38.210 --> 01:03:40.540
and I think the NEA should too.

01:03:40.540 --> 01:03:43.090
I'm also a populist
in that I believe

01:03:43.090 --> 01:03:45.880
that these standards
can and do make sense

01:03:45.880 --> 01:03:49.510
to people who are prepared
to pay enough attention.

01:03:49.510 --> 01:03:53.290
The surest way to convert
the NEA into a dead letter

01:03:53.290 --> 01:03:57.420
is to make its sponsorship an
area for battles, many of which

01:03:57.420 --> 01:03:59.760
do indeed need to be
fought, but fought

01:03:59.760 --> 01:04:01.929
in the sphere of politics.

01:04:01.929 --> 01:04:03.720
The trouble with fighting
political battles

01:04:03.720 --> 01:04:05.800
on cultural turf is twofold.

01:04:05.800 --> 01:04:09.300
First, as many of us will
recall from the 1960s,

01:04:09.300 --> 01:04:11.920
they are rarely won there.

01:04:11.920 --> 01:04:14.500
And secondly, in
the process, you

01:04:14.500 --> 01:04:17.480
may simply kill the grass
so that everybody loses.

01:04:17.480 --> 01:04:18.982
Thank you very much.

01:04:18.982 --> 01:04:23.942
[APPLAUSE]

01:05:07.094 --> 01:05:08.640
HARRIS: Are there any questions?

01:05:08.640 --> 01:05:11.740
[LAUGHING]

01:05:11.740 --> 01:05:14.730
Yes, please come down to the
microphone so we can hear you.

01:05:14.730 --> 01:05:16.454
Tell us who you are.

01:05:16.454 --> 01:05:21.921
AUDIENCE: My name is Williams,
and I live here in Cambridge.

01:05:21.921 --> 01:05:25.620
Two questions in
response to your talk.

01:05:25.620 --> 01:05:33.120
One is, you got into a bit
about the question of elitism.

01:05:33.120 --> 01:05:34.952
In discussion here
tonight with someone

01:05:34.952 --> 01:05:36.618
who I consider to be
a very good artist,

01:05:36.618 --> 01:05:40.530
he described the Boston
Symphony as basically preserving

01:05:40.530 --> 01:05:44.166
music that was once
performed for kings

01:05:44.166 --> 01:05:49.478
for today's equivalent,
only it's subsidized by--

01:05:49.478 --> 01:05:55.040
there's a subsidy for
the wealthy people

01:05:55.040 --> 01:06:00.500
primarily so they can
go and enjoy this music.

01:06:00.500 --> 01:06:04.167
And so I wonder if
you could speak to

01:06:04.167 --> 01:06:07.436
not whether it is
good or worthy,

01:06:07.436 --> 01:06:12.720
but whether it
should be subsidized.

01:06:12.720 --> 01:06:17.486
Maybe it should be paid for by
the people who are enjoying it.

01:06:17.486 --> 01:06:22.759
HUGHES: Who supposes that the
audience at the Boston Symph

01:06:22.759 --> 01:06:25.586
would consist exclusively
of the wealthy?

01:06:25.586 --> 01:06:26.252
AUDIENCE: Right.

01:06:26.252 --> 01:06:28.248
No, I understand.

01:06:28.248 --> 01:06:31.384
I don't think it is-- actually
they do have $6 rush tickets,

01:06:31.384 --> 01:06:32.300
and that was part of--

01:06:32.300 --> 01:06:34.326
HUGHES: Yes, well,
it doesn't seem to me

01:06:34.326 --> 01:06:36.700
that you need to be an unusually
wealthy person to afford

01:06:36.700 --> 01:06:38.032
a ticket to the Boston Symph.

01:06:42.229 --> 01:06:44.770
I would have thought that your
acquaintance could have better

01:06:44.770 --> 01:06:47.729
phrased it by saying that
they were taking music which,

01:06:47.729 --> 01:06:49.270
some of which at
any rate-- you know,

01:06:49.270 --> 01:06:51.910
it's not true that all the
music that the Boston Symphony

01:06:51.910 --> 01:06:53.770
performs or any other
symphony performs

01:06:53.770 --> 01:06:56.219
was actually written
at the behest of kings.

01:06:56.219 --> 01:06:58.510
But some of which, let's say,
for the sake of argument,

01:06:58.510 --> 01:07:00.902
was performed by kings,
and recycling it back

01:07:00.902 --> 01:07:02.110
to people who were not kings.

01:07:02.110 --> 01:07:03.484
In other words,
recycling it back

01:07:03.484 --> 01:07:06.070
to the citizens of the
democracy, rich and poor.

01:07:06.070 --> 01:07:09.880
I mean, it seems
to me profoundly

01:07:09.880 --> 01:07:13.360
condescending to suppose
that the only people who

01:07:13.360 --> 01:07:15.210
are interested in what
is, for convenience,

01:07:15.210 --> 01:07:17.290
called high culture
are the rich.

01:07:17.290 --> 01:07:17.930
It is not true.

01:07:20.810 --> 01:07:22.990
And if it were
true, every musical,

01:07:22.990 --> 01:07:26.520
every symphony hall in America
would have closed long ago.

01:07:26.520 --> 01:07:29.820
If classical music didn't have
some kind of popular base,

01:07:29.820 --> 01:07:32.448
it couldn't survive.

01:07:32.448 --> 01:07:34.744
AUDIENCE: The other question
I wanted to ask about

01:07:34.744 --> 01:07:37.368
was, in my experience
in Cambridge

01:07:37.368 --> 01:07:40.320
with Cambridge as
an Arts Council,

01:07:40.320 --> 01:07:44.872
this was another criticism
that was levied against--

01:07:44.872 --> 01:07:49.030
well, the problems of
funding of the arts

01:07:49.030 --> 01:07:52.515
is that the sense that,
at least on a local level,

01:07:52.515 --> 01:07:55.610
maybe not as much
on a national level,

01:07:55.610 --> 01:07:59.090
that it becomes a kind
of arts bureaucracy

01:07:59.090 --> 01:08:01.630
where arts bureaucrats,
not artists,

01:08:01.630 --> 01:08:05.740
who have a regular
staple of favorites

01:08:05.740 --> 01:08:08.820
that they fund year after
year who aren't, in fact,

01:08:08.820 --> 01:08:09.320
excellent--

01:08:09.320 --> 01:08:12.610
HUGHES: Well, I think there's
probably a good deal of merit

01:08:12.610 --> 01:08:14.050
to that criticism.

01:08:14.050 --> 01:08:19.300
There is always-- one of the
great problems of setting up

01:08:19.300 --> 01:08:24.069
arts founding systems is to
avoid losses by log rolling,

01:08:24.069 --> 01:08:26.710
by favoritism, by
backscratching,

01:08:26.710 --> 01:08:30.319
and simply by
bureaucratic friction.

01:08:30.319 --> 01:08:36.760
And I think one of the inherent
problems of the NEA peer review

01:08:36.760 --> 01:08:41.830
system is that you get chaps
and chapesses scratching

01:08:41.830 --> 01:08:43.250
one another's back.

01:08:43.250 --> 01:08:44.920
But when you think
of the alternative

01:08:44.920 --> 01:08:47.920
to it, all the various
alternatives to it,

01:08:47.920 --> 01:08:49.840
I think probably there
is still quite a lot

01:08:49.840 --> 01:08:52.000
to be said for the
peer review system.

01:08:52.000 --> 01:08:54.819
But the idea that--

01:08:54.819 --> 01:08:58.189
alas, we do not live
in a Utopian world.

01:08:58.189 --> 01:09:01.830
And it has never been
possible to devise--

01:09:01.830 --> 01:09:04.390
I think probably
not since the time

01:09:04.390 --> 01:09:06.680
that Aug and Maug first
did their first buffalo

01:09:06.680 --> 01:09:07.930
on the Orignation Caves.

01:09:07.930 --> 01:09:10.810
It has not been possible to
devise a system of arts funding

01:09:10.810 --> 01:09:13.460
that totally eliminated
favoritism and log rolling.

01:09:13.460 --> 01:09:15.500
Alas.

01:09:15.500 --> 01:09:17.560
But the question
is, because of that,

01:09:17.560 --> 01:09:19.810
because arts funding
does not represent

01:09:19.810 --> 01:09:24.529
the sort of mechanisms of
an ideal cultural world,

01:09:24.529 --> 01:09:26.029
do you therefore
dispensing with it?

01:09:26.029 --> 01:09:30.399
I mean, I shudder to think of
the number of really rather

01:09:30.399 --> 01:09:35.450
mediocre works of art that
have been commissioned

01:09:35.450 --> 01:09:37.540
in France in the 19th century.

01:09:37.540 --> 01:09:39.520
Quite a few.

01:09:39.520 --> 01:09:43.359
And endless protests from
artists and complaints

01:09:43.359 --> 01:09:48.170
from artists about the laxity,
the blindness, the deafness,

01:09:48.170 --> 01:09:50.920
the general unresponsiveness
of cultural bureaucrats.

01:09:50.920 --> 01:09:54.820
I mean, you read the
journals of Hector Berlioz,

01:09:54.820 --> 01:09:57.670
for instance, one sees
it at full stretch.

01:09:57.670 --> 01:09:59.570
Nevertheless, on
the ballots, I think

01:09:59.570 --> 01:10:04.420
one is grateful in the end that
there was some kind of system

01:10:04.420 --> 01:10:07.870
set up for the public
encouragement of the arts,

01:10:07.870 --> 01:10:08.560
on the results.

01:10:15.186 --> 01:10:16.810
AUDIENCE: I wondered
what your thoughts

01:10:16.810 --> 01:10:19.945
were about the idea of
artists suing the NEA.

01:10:19.945 --> 01:10:21.430
This happened recently.

01:10:21.430 --> 01:10:24.265
A number of artists
and performers--

01:10:24.265 --> 01:10:25.390
HUGHES: Well, it's America.

01:10:25.390 --> 01:10:26.710
Everybody sues everybody else.

01:10:31.000 --> 01:10:33.230
This is the most litigious
society that ever existed,

01:10:33.230 --> 01:10:33.730
I think.

01:10:39.244 --> 01:10:40.660
I have to tell you
quite honestly,

01:10:40.660 --> 01:10:44.710
I haven't closely
followed the ins and outs

01:10:44.710 --> 01:10:46.540
of the case that was
brought, I think,

01:10:46.540 --> 01:10:51.850
by Karen Finley,
and Holly Hughes,

01:10:51.850 --> 01:10:54.160
and others against the NEA.

01:10:54.160 --> 01:10:57.400
I mean, my first reaction
was, oh, come on.

01:10:57.400 --> 01:10:59.300
Give me a break.

01:10:59.300 --> 01:11:02.710
But it revolves
around the question

01:11:02.710 --> 01:11:05.110
of whether active censorship
did indeed take place,

01:11:05.110 --> 01:11:07.570
and I think the court
concluded that it did so.

01:11:07.570 --> 01:11:09.145
But I'm afraid that I haven't--

01:11:09.145 --> 01:11:11.020
rather to my shame, I
have not followed that.

01:11:11.020 --> 01:11:12.220
I've not given
that the attention

01:11:12.220 --> 01:11:14.345
that I should have because
I was working on a book.

01:11:14.345 --> 01:11:16.570
And so consequently, I'm
sorry to cop out like this.

01:11:16.570 --> 01:11:21.880
I really do feel that it is
hard to talk about people's

01:11:21.880 --> 01:11:23.650
entitlements to a grant.

01:11:23.650 --> 01:11:26.830
Nobody is entitled to
a government grant.

01:11:26.830 --> 01:11:29.230
I think this is a spin-off
from an excessive kind

01:11:29.230 --> 01:11:31.480
of caricatured
sense of entitlement

01:11:31.480 --> 01:11:35.060
that runs through
America at the moment.

01:11:35.060 --> 01:11:36.940
HARRIS: Could you
tell us who you are?

01:11:36.940 --> 01:11:38.606
AUDIENCE: My name is
Larry [INAUDIBLE]..

01:11:38.606 --> 01:11:40.538
I'm from Prague.

01:11:40.538 --> 01:11:41.504
HARRIS: Thank you.

01:11:41.504 --> 01:11:42.953
AUDIENCE: My name
is Liam Rector.

01:11:42.953 --> 01:11:44.402
I'm covering this for
The Harvard Review,

01:11:44.402 --> 01:11:45.851
and I teach at
Bennington College.

01:11:45.851 --> 01:11:48.266
I understood you,
Mr. Hughes, that you

01:11:48.266 --> 01:11:50.107
must have read John
Frohnmayer's book,

01:11:50.107 --> 01:11:51.190
Getting Out of Time Alive?

01:11:51.190 --> 01:11:52.362
HUGHES: Yes, I did.

01:11:52.362 --> 01:11:54.322
AUDIENCE: It's, in my
view, a pitiful book.

01:11:54.322 --> 01:11:55.488
HUGHES: Yes, in my view too.

01:11:55.488 --> 01:11:56.096
AUDIENCE: I'd like
to know thoughts

01:11:56.096 --> 01:11:57.096
on the book itself, one.

01:11:57.096 --> 01:12:00.000
And two, I'd like
to know what sort

01:12:00.000 --> 01:12:04.530
do you think would be a good
NEA director, both in general,

01:12:04.530 --> 01:12:06.955
and some specific examples
you have might have--

01:12:06.955 --> 01:12:07.705
HUGHES: Oh, well--

01:12:07.705 --> 01:12:09.130
AUDIENCE: --of somebody
that would lead the NEA,

01:12:09.130 --> 01:12:09.610
for example.

01:12:09.610 --> 01:12:10.770
HUGHES: --obviously,
you need a woman

01:12:10.770 --> 01:12:13.380
with the reach of a gorilla and
the balls of a brass kangaroo.

01:12:13.380 --> 01:12:17.040
[LAUGHING]

01:12:20.580 --> 01:12:23.204
Frohnmayer fulfilled none
of these requirements.

01:12:27.494 --> 01:12:29.160
I mean, Frohnmayer
is a very nice bloke.

01:12:33.185 --> 01:12:34.980
He hasn't got a mean
bone in his body.

01:12:34.980 --> 01:12:39.170
That's why his tenure
at the NEA was such a--

01:12:39.170 --> 01:12:41.850
was so bedeviled.

01:12:41.850 --> 01:12:46.410
In order to resist the kind
of political opportunism

01:12:46.410 --> 01:12:48.002
that is thumping upon the--

01:12:48.002 --> 01:12:50.460
I mean, you know, there are
various grounds for criticizing

01:12:50.460 --> 01:12:53.280
the NEA, which range
from, shall we say,

01:12:53.280 --> 01:12:55.770
at least an intelligent
conservative view

01:12:55.770 --> 01:12:59.280
of the matter to this kind
of lumping opportunism,

01:12:59.280 --> 01:13:03.660
which we see from the
right Republicans.

01:13:03.660 --> 01:13:05.580
They're just basically
grandstanding

01:13:05.580 --> 01:13:08.580
and don't give a damn about
what the cultural health

01:13:08.580 --> 01:13:11.950
of this country might
or might not be.

01:13:11.950 --> 01:13:18.000
And in order to resist this, one
needs an extraordinary amount--

01:13:18.000 --> 01:13:19.915
you just need to
be extremely tough.

01:13:19.915 --> 01:13:21.510
Frohnmayer wasn't
tough, nor do I

01:13:21.510 --> 01:13:24.780
believe that he had his
fundamental cultural priorities

01:13:24.780 --> 01:13:25.620
in the right place.

01:13:25.620 --> 01:13:27.304
He came into there
not quite knowing

01:13:27.304 --> 01:13:29.220
what he thought, brandishing
his little cello,

01:13:29.220 --> 01:13:32.464
or whatever the hell
it was he plays.

01:13:32.464 --> 01:13:33.630
No, there are little cellos.

01:13:33.630 --> 01:13:35.690
I'm sorry.

01:13:35.690 --> 01:13:40.070
And he was like a
fish out of water.

01:13:40.070 --> 01:13:44.495
And the subtitle of his book--

01:13:44.495 --> 01:13:45.660
what was it?

01:13:45.660 --> 01:13:48.530
Memoirs or Confessions of
an Arts Warrior, you know,

01:13:48.530 --> 01:13:50.550
which suggests some
brave fellow going

01:13:50.550 --> 01:13:52.150
forth with his magic
sword to conquer

01:13:52.150 --> 01:13:53.554
the Mordor of Washington.

01:13:53.554 --> 01:13:54.720
Well, of course, it's hokum.

01:13:57.270 --> 01:14:00.690
He was the wrong guy
in the wrong place,

01:14:00.690 --> 01:14:04.710
just as I would say Anne-Imela
Radice was the wrong person

01:14:04.710 --> 01:14:07.570
in the wrong place.

01:14:07.570 --> 01:14:11.310
And anybody who
goes into running

01:14:11.310 --> 01:14:14.090
the NEA on the terms which
were dictated for Radice

01:14:14.090 --> 01:14:17.340
is also going to be the wrong
person in the wrong place.

01:14:17.340 --> 01:14:22.620
Because if you have a
supposedly independent system

01:14:22.620 --> 01:14:25.200
of arts funding, which then
becomes buffeted around

01:14:25.200 --> 01:14:28.650
on the whims of whatever
the electoral requirements

01:14:28.650 --> 01:14:32.230
of the moment might
be, as the NEA was

01:14:32.230 --> 01:14:38.259
during Frohnmayer's and
Radice's tenure, then

01:14:38.259 --> 01:14:40.050
the institution is not
well-served by those

01:14:40.050 --> 01:14:40.620
who run it.

01:14:40.620 --> 01:14:42.049
Yes, sir?

01:14:42.049 --> 01:14:43.090
AUDIENCE: This gentlemen.

01:14:43.090 --> 01:14:43.430
HUGHES: Oh, I'm sorry.

01:14:43.430 --> 01:14:44.374
Here.

01:14:44.374 --> 01:14:44.915
AUDIENCE: Hi.

01:14:44.915 --> 01:14:47.885
My name's Sam Walker, and I'm
with The Christian Science

01:14:47.885 --> 01:14:48.720
Monitor.

01:14:48.720 --> 01:14:52.600
And my question is, in an
age of extreme pressure

01:14:52.600 --> 01:14:55.490
to reduce government spending,
what would your strongest

01:14:55.490 --> 01:14:59.513
argument be to the
appropriations committee

01:14:59.513 --> 01:15:04.644
to either maintain or
increase funding for the NEA?

01:15:04.644 --> 01:15:06.060
HUGHES: I think
it would be rather

01:15:06.060 --> 01:15:08.650
difficult to make the argument
for increasing its funding

01:15:08.650 --> 01:15:09.150
to fly.

01:15:09.150 --> 01:15:15.810
Although, I do actually believe
that the NEA would probably not

01:15:15.810 --> 01:15:18.480
even do better if it had
more money at its disposal.

01:15:18.480 --> 01:15:21.060
The argument that I would make
is a very straightforward one.

01:15:24.900 --> 01:15:29.430
Really, it is that some
degree of government

01:15:29.430 --> 01:15:36.230
support for works of
art, be they concerts,

01:15:36.230 --> 01:15:40.430
or theater, or
whatever, performance

01:15:40.430 --> 01:15:45.650
of any kind, or
paintings, or sculpture,

01:15:45.650 --> 01:15:50.990
is actually necessary
in a society

01:15:50.990 --> 01:15:55.410
where not everything
that is good

01:15:55.410 --> 01:16:03.170
can depend automatically
upon private or corporate

01:16:03.170 --> 01:16:05.580
institutional support.

01:16:05.580 --> 01:16:08.550
The second argument is that
the main function of the NEA,

01:16:08.550 --> 01:16:10.380
as I see it, is to
act as a catalyst

01:16:10.380 --> 01:16:14.880
towards institutional
support for genuinely

01:16:14.880 --> 01:16:18.079
informative and useful
kinds of cultural--

01:16:18.079 --> 01:16:19.620
I hate the word
cultural programming,

01:16:19.620 --> 01:16:20.460
which sounds stern.

01:16:20.460 --> 01:16:22.440
You know, cultural events.

01:16:22.440 --> 01:16:24.320
You know, the
sustenance of museums,

01:16:24.320 --> 01:16:27.690
the sustenance of
symphony orchestras

01:16:27.690 --> 01:16:36.270
and so forth, which actually,
after the over optimistic

01:16:36.270 --> 01:16:39.516
projections made in the '80s
about how everything could be

01:16:39.516 --> 01:16:40.890
take, or all this
kind of support

01:16:40.890 --> 01:16:42.556
could be taken up by
the private sector,

01:16:42.556 --> 01:16:45.060
it has been fairly well
shown that, actually, it's

01:16:45.060 --> 01:16:48.600
not so easy for the private
sector to come in fearlessly

01:16:48.600 --> 01:16:49.100
and do it.

01:16:49.100 --> 01:16:50.891
It needs a certain
amount of encouragement.

01:16:50.891 --> 01:16:52.230
It needs to be led by the nose.

01:16:52.230 --> 01:16:54.960
The NEA has always had a
very important function

01:16:54.960 --> 01:16:58.380
in this relationship.

01:16:58.380 --> 01:17:02.040
I defend the NEA primarily
as catalyst and only

01:17:02.040 --> 01:17:04.740
in the second place as patron.

01:17:04.740 --> 01:17:07.350
And I would say to
people who are calling

01:17:07.350 --> 01:17:11.400
for a diminishment of its funds
that these funds are already

01:17:11.400 --> 01:17:12.720
cut to the bone.

01:17:12.720 --> 01:17:17.580
They are by comparison to
other great democratic states

01:17:17.580 --> 01:17:21.300
in Europe almost
ludicrously low,

01:17:21.300 --> 01:17:26.550
and that their present
expenditure does not

01:17:26.550 --> 01:17:31.690
involve any serious drainage
from other vital parts

01:17:31.690 --> 01:17:33.330
of national interest.

01:17:33.330 --> 01:17:36.059
So why not keep it going?

01:17:36.059 --> 01:17:37.350
AUDIENCE: If I could follow up.

01:17:37.350 --> 01:17:41.045
There have been some arguments
that in order to be a catalyst,

01:17:41.045 --> 01:17:45.220
the NEA doesn't necessarily
have to send any money in,

01:17:45.220 --> 01:17:46.897
that the stamp of
approval from the NEA

01:17:46.897 --> 01:17:50.470
doesn't necessarily
constitute spending.

01:17:50.470 --> 01:17:52.269
How would you respond to that?

01:17:52.269 --> 01:17:54.310
HUGHES: Oh, I think there's
no sign of commitment

01:17:54.310 --> 01:17:56.080
quite like a check.

01:17:56.080 --> 01:17:58.780
[LAUGHING]

01:17:58.780 --> 01:17:59.910
Words is cheap.

01:18:03.782 --> 01:18:04.750
Yes?

01:18:04.750 --> 01:18:06.363
AUDIENCE: My name is Roger Kahn.

01:18:06.363 --> 01:18:08.862
I'm a professor, and I'm
the Director for the Center

01:18:08.862 --> 01:18:10.570
for Non-Profit
Organization of Leadership

01:18:10.570 --> 01:18:13.662
at Regis University in Denver
and the program chairman

01:18:13.662 --> 01:18:17.351
of a forthcoming conference
on arts, culture,

01:18:17.351 --> 01:18:22.370
and community public funding
and social responsibility.

01:18:22.370 --> 01:18:25.660
Your comments on the Dutch
experience and your observation

01:18:25.660 --> 01:18:28.870
that the goal of the
democracy of the arts

01:18:28.870 --> 01:18:31.602
is to make the world
safe from elitism

01:18:31.602 --> 01:18:34.592
made me wonder what
you, in fact, think

01:18:34.592 --> 01:18:37.960
whether the NEA and other
public funding sources

01:18:37.960 --> 01:18:42.966
are suitable criteria for
supporting public arts.

01:18:42.966 --> 01:18:45.090
HUGHES: Whether I think
they have suitable criteria

01:18:45.090 --> 01:18:46.630
or they do have criteria?

01:18:46.630 --> 01:18:48.505
AUDIENCE: Not whether
you do think they have,

01:18:48.505 --> 01:18:50.260
but what you think
they might be.

01:18:52.906 --> 01:18:57.370
HUGHES: One is often asked about
what criteria should be assumed

01:18:57.370 --> 01:19:02.230
by the NEA as if there were a
kind of a yardstick which that

01:19:02.230 --> 01:19:05.590
august institution could put out
of its pocket and lay alongside

01:19:05.590 --> 01:19:06.250
a work of art.

01:19:06.250 --> 01:19:09.790
Basically, what the NEA does,
and what I think it should do,

01:19:09.790 --> 01:19:13.000
is make the kind of educated
guesses about sensibility

01:19:13.000 --> 01:19:17.170
and its action about
creative sensibility

01:19:17.170 --> 01:19:22.480
that you or I might make as
sort of literate observers

01:19:22.480 --> 01:19:26.470
of whatever art we like,
and then go ahead and do it

01:19:26.470 --> 01:19:27.500
on that basis.

01:19:27.500 --> 01:19:30.220
In other words, I tried
earlier in the speech

01:19:30.220 --> 01:19:34.400
to outline very strongly, you
know, to argue very strongly,

01:19:34.400 --> 01:19:36.580
that in the first
place, the NEA should

01:19:36.580 --> 01:19:41.560
be looking for some sort
of genuine and arguable

01:19:41.560 --> 01:19:46.470
excellence, hopefully in the
results that it subsidizes.

01:19:46.470 --> 01:19:48.091
But that excellence
could only be

01:19:48.091 --> 01:19:49.840
argued in the presence
of the work of art.

01:19:49.840 --> 01:19:52.420
This is one of the big
problems with funding things

01:19:52.420 --> 01:19:55.580
before they actually take place.

01:19:55.580 --> 01:19:57.340
You see the results afterwards.

01:19:57.340 --> 01:20:01.270
And it may work or not.

01:20:01.270 --> 01:20:03.640
But basically, what
they should be doing

01:20:03.640 --> 01:20:08.290
is looking for serious
talent within the field

01:20:08.290 --> 01:20:11.140
that they wish to
subsidize and going

01:20:11.140 --> 01:20:15.040
on whatever aesthetic criteria
they believe to be proper

01:20:15.040 --> 01:20:16.270
and which can be argued.

01:20:16.270 --> 01:20:18.443
And I believe they
should be argued.

01:20:18.443 --> 01:20:20.484
AUDIENCE: If I can push
that for just one second,

01:20:20.484 --> 01:20:24.000
it seems to me that if
Picasso was incarnated,

01:20:24.000 --> 01:20:26.524
we might find that he
would worthy of support

01:20:26.524 --> 01:20:28.095
because of his prior record.

01:20:28.095 --> 01:20:29.720
HUGHES: Yes, we know
that he's Picasso.

01:20:29.720 --> 01:20:31.428
AUDIENCE: I listen to
what you're saying.

01:20:31.428 --> 01:20:35.162
And in the context of the
pluralistic democracy, where

01:20:35.162 --> 01:20:38.480
there are so many people
that we are not familiar,

01:20:38.480 --> 01:20:42.085
and so many groups with
which we are not familiar,

01:20:42.085 --> 01:20:44.560
are there any standards
that you can think of

01:20:44.560 --> 01:20:46.045
or criteria that
you can articulate

01:20:46.045 --> 01:20:49.592
that would help us deliver
the process of figuring out

01:20:49.592 --> 01:20:53.192
what it is we want to be
supporting of with money.

01:20:53.192 --> 01:20:54.650
HUGHES: You go for
the work of art.

01:20:54.650 --> 01:20:57.920
You go for the person or
the group's past record.

01:20:57.920 --> 01:21:00.260
You look as
attentively as you can

01:21:00.260 --> 01:21:02.480
at what that person
or group has done.

01:21:02.480 --> 01:21:04.200
You try to figure
out, therefore,

01:21:04.200 --> 01:21:07.250
you know, on that basis
whether that talent

01:21:07.250 --> 01:21:12.980
has the kind of coherence and
adventurousness spirit and sort

01:21:12.980 --> 01:21:17.757
of intelligence of
results that one is

01:21:17.757 --> 01:21:18.965
looking for in a work of art.

01:21:22.650 --> 01:21:25.710
As I say, it is hard to sort
of invent a verbal three foot

01:21:25.710 --> 01:21:32.340
rule, which you can then lay
alongside a play or a painting

01:21:32.340 --> 01:21:34.600
in advance and say, OK,
this measures up to it.

01:21:34.600 --> 01:21:36.010
This doesn't.

01:21:36.010 --> 01:21:38.989
One's understanding
of a work of art

01:21:38.989 --> 01:21:40.530
is essentially an
existential matter.

01:21:40.530 --> 01:21:42.480
It happens in the
presence of the work.

01:21:42.480 --> 01:21:44.991
You get some sort of
a visceral reaction.

01:21:44.991 --> 01:21:45.990
And it's that, you know?

01:21:45.990 --> 01:21:48.840
It's the ability of the
thing to, I suppose,

01:21:48.840 --> 01:21:55.680
spark that promotes the small
act of faith in the artist.

01:21:55.680 --> 01:21:57.900
In other words, it is
a subjective judgment.

01:21:57.900 --> 01:22:02.780
You cannot escape subjective
judgments by multiplying

01:22:02.780 --> 01:22:04.530
subjectivities in the
form of a committee.

01:22:04.530 --> 01:22:06.486
It still ends up as a
subjective judgment,

01:22:06.486 --> 01:22:07.860
and it has to be
pursued as such.

01:22:13.294 --> 01:22:14.776
AUDIENCE: Oh, I'm sorry.

01:22:18.234 --> 01:22:20.704
AUDIENCE: Since you talked
about multiculturalism--

01:22:20.704 --> 01:22:22.680
Patty Martin with
The Boston Globe.

01:22:22.680 --> 01:22:24.656
HUGHES: How do you do?

01:22:24.656 --> 01:22:28.114
AUDIENCE: --say that
issues of multiculturalism

01:22:28.114 --> 01:22:30.090
should not be played
out in the National

01:22:30.090 --> 01:22:30.584
Endowment for the Arts?

01:22:30.584 --> 01:22:32.850
HUGHES: No, I didn't say
issues of multiculturalism

01:22:32.850 --> 01:22:33.220
shouldn't be.

01:22:33.220 --> 01:22:34.920
I said issues of group
entitlement shouldn't be.

01:22:34.920 --> 01:22:36.502
There's a very
important distinction.

01:22:36.502 --> 01:22:37.386
AUDIENCE: Well,
then, I'm curious,

01:22:37.386 --> 01:22:38.712
what do you think
cultural institutions

01:22:38.712 --> 01:22:39.420
should be doing about this?

01:22:39.420 --> 01:22:40.761
HUGHES: They should
be encouraging talent.

01:22:40.761 --> 01:22:43.470
AUDIENCE: Even with demographics
that we don't know about?

01:22:43.470 --> 01:22:44.730
HUGHES: They should
be encouraging talent,

01:22:44.730 --> 01:22:47.160
and talent it is not necessarily
a function of demographics.

01:22:47.160 --> 01:22:49.201
They should be looking as
widely as they possibly

01:22:49.201 --> 01:22:52.800
can across the
demographic spectrum.

01:22:52.800 --> 01:22:55.030
That goes without saying.

01:22:55.030 --> 01:22:58.424
But in the last
analysis, when it

01:22:58.424 --> 01:23:00.090
comes down to what
you're going to fund,

01:23:00.090 --> 01:23:01.923
you're funded on the
basis of what you think

01:23:01.923 --> 01:23:03.120
is good, the work I meant.

01:23:06.420 --> 01:23:09.130
They shouldn't be having
quotas for blacks or gays

01:23:09.130 --> 01:23:11.350
or women or whites or whatever.

01:23:11.350 --> 01:23:13.380
There should be no such
quotas in my opinion.

01:23:13.380 --> 01:23:14.240
No.

01:23:14.240 --> 01:23:17.290
But there should be a
very definite openness

01:23:17.290 --> 01:23:21.160
to all those groups and
to all sectors of society,

01:23:21.160 --> 01:23:23.500
and there should be
a profound curiosity

01:23:23.500 --> 01:23:24.557
about what they're doing.

01:23:24.557 --> 01:23:26.140
And then at the end
of the day, you've

01:23:26.140 --> 01:23:30.340
still got to make your judgment
on the basis of whether you

01:23:30.340 --> 01:23:32.384
think there's real
talent there or not.

01:23:32.384 --> 01:23:33.800
AUDIENCE: This
gentlemen, I think,

01:23:33.800 --> 01:23:36.250
was trying to get at
questions of standards.

01:23:36.250 --> 01:23:40.660
Do you think standards differ
from committee to committee?

01:23:40.660 --> 01:23:46.060
I mean, one might wanna look
at ancient period itself--

01:23:46.060 --> 01:23:48.370
HUGHES: Well, it depends on
what you mean by standards.

01:23:48.370 --> 01:23:51.899
If, as many people
think, you know,

01:23:51.899 --> 01:23:53.940
there are hermetically
sealed different standards

01:23:53.940 --> 01:23:56.500
from community to
community, why is it

01:23:56.500 --> 01:23:58.540
that you or I could
go into a museum

01:23:58.540 --> 01:24:02.050
and look at a work of art
from a culture immensely

01:24:02.050 --> 01:24:08.470
separated from us in space and
time and cultural assumptions,

01:24:08.470 --> 01:24:13.330
such as, for instance, a bronze
pot from the Chu dynasty,

01:24:13.330 --> 01:24:19.330
and find it absolutely radiant
with meaning and beauty?

01:24:19.330 --> 01:24:22.834
My view is that the idea of
differing aesthetic standards

01:24:22.834 --> 01:24:24.250
from community to
community is one

01:24:24.250 --> 01:24:26.320
which has been tremendously
inflated in America

01:24:26.320 --> 01:24:29.260
and rather badly argued.

01:24:29.260 --> 01:24:35.780
In point of fact, there is
absolutely no reason on Earth

01:24:35.780 --> 01:24:41.180
that I can think of why,
for instance, a white reader

01:24:41.180 --> 01:24:43.820
wouldn't be able to get the
point of reading Langston

01:24:43.820 --> 01:24:47.900
Hughes, or why a black
onlooker wouldn't

01:24:47.900 --> 01:24:51.740
be able to get into the work
of Piero della Francesco

01:24:51.740 --> 01:24:54.492
just as much as a white one.

01:24:54.492 --> 01:24:57.040
I think there is far too--

01:24:57.040 --> 01:24:59.060
I think there is a
great deal of can't

01:24:59.060 --> 01:25:04.520
involved in the inflation
of the idea of differing

01:25:04.520 --> 01:25:09.510
aesthetic community standards
on aesthetics within America.

01:25:09.510 --> 01:25:11.760
But my point is, and
I've made it before,

01:25:11.760 --> 01:25:12.840
and I will make it again.

01:25:12.840 --> 01:25:16.410
It is absolutely essential
that funding bodies

01:25:16.410 --> 01:25:22.200
should look and pursue
their investigation of what

01:25:22.200 --> 01:25:25.740
is out there as
widely as possible.

01:25:25.740 --> 01:25:28.650
There must be no
group, no person,

01:25:28.650 --> 01:25:34.460
who is ineligible a priori
for this kind of support.

01:25:34.460 --> 01:25:39.035
AUDIENCE: Should the
bodies be represented?

01:25:39.035 --> 01:25:40.660
HUGHES: Well, I think
they already are.

01:25:40.660 --> 01:25:42.630
And yes, I think they should be.

01:25:42.630 --> 01:25:43.840
Yes.

01:25:43.840 --> 01:25:46.420
But again, I think it
is rather difficult

01:25:46.420 --> 01:25:52.180
to formulate recipes for this.

01:25:52.180 --> 01:25:57.820
You know, obviously, you don't
want monochrome or monosexual

01:25:57.820 --> 01:25:59.020
bodies determining this.

01:25:59.020 --> 01:25:59.890
You know, of course.

01:25:59.890 --> 01:26:02.110
I mean, it's a democracy.

01:26:02.110 --> 01:26:03.580
It should run
across the spectrum.

01:26:03.580 --> 01:26:08.260
But on the other hand, since
Americans at the moment

01:26:08.260 --> 01:26:10.270
are so fond of
dividing themselves

01:26:10.270 --> 01:26:13.540
into microconstituencies
and then claiming

01:26:13.540 --> 01:26:15.272
that each of these
microconstituencies

01:26:15.272 --> 01:26:17.230
represents a separate
culture, which very often

01:26:17.230 --> 01:26:22.084
they really don't,
there is a severe danger

01:26:22.084 --> 01:26:24.250
that that whole process can
bog down and become just

01:26:24.250 --> 01:26:25.900
vociferous and meaningless.

01:26:25.900 --> 01:26:28.880
But in the broader
spectrum, yes, I

01:26:28.880 --> 01:26:31.300
think it would be
ridiculous, for instance,

01:26:31.300 --> 01:26:36.250
to have some kind
of a requirement

01:26:36.250 --> 01:26:38.590
that each review
panel should contain

01:26:38.590 --> 01:26:41.530
70 gays and 70 straights.

01:26:41.530 --> 01:26:46.630
But on the much larger
divisions-- men,

01:26:46.630 --> 01:26:48.880
women, blacks, whites,
Hispanics, whatever-- yes,

01:26:48.880 --> 01:26:51.171
I think there's a very strong
case to be made for that.

01:26:51.171 --> 01:26:53.110
And in practice, it
works out that way.

01:26:53.110 --> 01:26:54.424
Yes?

01:26:54.424 --> 01:26:56.738
AUDIENCE: Caroline
[INAUDIBLE] from New York.

01:26:56.738 --> 01:26:58.196
Do you believe that
there should be

01:26:58.196 --> 01:27:01.810
a cabinet or at most a minister
of culture in the United

01:27:01.810 --> 01:27:02.310
States?

01:27:02.310 --> 01:27:04.780
HUGHES: No, I do not.

01:27:04.780 --> 01:27:05.280
No.

01:27:05.280 --> 01:27:07.050
I think it would
be a dreadful idea.

01:27:07.050 --> 01:27:08.910
Think of what you'd get.

01:27:08.910 --> 01:27:14.250
[LAUGHING]

01:27:14.250 --> 01:27:17.930
I mean, only Minister of Culture
that any of us can imagine

01:27:17.930 --> 01:27:20.572
would be ourselves.

01:27:20.572 --> 01:27:22.460
You know?

01:27:22.460 --> 01:27:24.470
We would all, on
a certain level,

01:27:24.470 --> 01:27:26.390
like to be the
Minister de la Couture.

01:27:26.390 --> 01:27:27.690
But it ain't going to happen.

01:27:27.690 --> 01:27:29.750
And if it did happen,
it would be somebody

01:27:29.750 --> 01:27:31.362
who would be sort of just--

01:27:31.362 --> 01:27:33.070
you know, would turn
it into a nightmare.

01:27:33.070 --> 01:27:38.470
Don't forget-- I mean,
it would become such a--

01:27:38.470 --> 01:27:42.500
the way in which the NEA
became a partisan football

01:27:42.500 --> 01:27:44.510
over the last few
years I promise

01:27:44.510 --> 01:27:46.910
you would be nothing compared
to the results of having

01:27:46.910 --> 01:27:50.280
a Minister of Culture
in this democracy.

01:27:50.280 --> 01:27:55.580
I don't believe you see
that actually it works out--

01:27:55.580 --> 01:27:57.540
I don't think it's
altogether consistent

01:27:57.540 --> 01:28:02.640
with the democratic
spirit of America, which

01:28:02.640 --> 01:28:04.460
we foreigners so much admire.

01:28:04.460 --> 01:28:06.960
I mean, I really wouldn't want
to see an American Jack Lang.

01:28:09.980 --> 01:28:11.710
And Jack Lang, in
a certain sense,

01:28:11.710 --> 01:28:13.414
was the American Jack Lang.

01:28:13.414 --> 01:28:17.860
[LAUGHING]

01:28:17.860 --> 01:28:18.369
Sir?

01:28:18.369 --> 01:28:18.952
AUDIENCE: Yes.

01:28:18.952 --> 01:28:21.452
My name is Jim-- just a person.

01:28:21.452 --> 01:28:24.190
I grew up outside of Boston
in a sort of suburban town.

01:28:24.190 --> 01:28:25.312
HUGHES: Me too.

01:28:25.312 --> 01:28:27.160
[LAUGHING]

01:28:27.160 --> 01:28:28.996
AUDIENCE: I was
wondering, not only

01:28:28.996 --> 01:28:30.954
in short-term, how they
should be administered,

01:28:30.954 --> 01:28:36.105
or how public support should
be directed to long-term?

01:28:36.105 --> 01:28:37.646
You know, it's only
the last 30 years

01:28:37.646 --> 01:28:39.930
that the NEA has even existed.

01:28:39.930 --> 01:28:42.737
More to the point is whether
we should have any at all.

01:28:42.737 --> 01:28:43.320
HUGHES: Sorry.

01:28:43.320 --> 01:28:44.600
Whether we should have
any at all of what?

01:28:44.600 --> 01:28:45.460
AUDIENCE: Public support.

01:28:45.460 --> 01:28:45.750
HUGHES: Yes.

01:28:45.750 --> 01:28:46.333
AUDIENCE: Yes?

01:28:46.333 --> 01:28:48.540
HUGHES: Yeah.

01:28:48.540 --> 01:28:51.000
It's always been a subject
of very divisive debate

01:28:51.000 --> 01:28:52.810
in America, whether
you should have it.

01:28:52.810 --> 01:28:55.780
AUDIENCE: You know, I think
about it in my suburban town,

01:28:55.780 --> 01:28:58.140
when public funds for
schooling and education

01:28:58.140 --> 01:28:59.011
started to dry up--

01:28:59.011 --> 01:28:59.510
HUGHES: Yes.

01:28:59.510 --> 01:29:01.259
AUDIENCE: --one of the
first things people

01:29:01.259 --> 01:29:04.500
think that is disposable
is art class or art space.

01:29:04.500 --> 01:29:06.210
HUGHES: Unfortunately, yes.

01:29:06.210 --> 01:29:07.626
AUDIENCE: It just
makes me think--

01:29:07.626 --> 01:29:10.180
it makes me wonder how
much basic interest America

01:29:10.180 --> 01:29:12.950
as a culture has in art.

01:29:12.950 --> 01:29:14.852
Is that any lower than
it is in other place?

01:29:14.852 --> 01:29:17.620
What is your opinion about what
our fundamental basic interest

01:29:17.620 --> 01:29:18.120
is in this?

01:29:18.120 --> 01:29:19.020
HUGHES: See, I
think, for instance,

01:29:19.020 --> 01:29:21.641
that art classes in schools can
have a very powerful educative

01:29:21.641 --> 01:29:22.140
effect.

01:29:22.140 --> 01:29:24.990
You know, they can be
terrific for the kids,

01:29:24.990 --> 01:29:27.800
and not just because it's
teaching a finger-painting,

01:29:27.800 --> 01:29:32.460
but because it connects them
to a whole mode of experiencing

01:29:32.460 --> 01:29:34.800
the world which they
would not have otherwise.

01:29:34.800 --> 01:29:42.000
And the idea that, in some way,
classes which expose the kids

01:29:42.000 --> 01:29:46.410
to painting, music, dance,
whatever it might be, theatre,

01:29:46.410 --> 01:29:49.590
is in some way optional, I
think it's a tremendous pity.

01:29:49.590 --> 01:29:51.280
Looking back on
my own education,

01:29:51.280 --> 01:29:54.810
which was certainly a very much
more classical and rigorous--

01:29:54.810 --> 01:29:58.140
I wouldn't say anti-art, but it
just ignored the plastic arts

01:29:58.140 --> 01:30:00.480
completely, I might say.

01:30:00.480 --> 01:30:03.320
But you know there was--

01:30:03.320 --> 01:30:06.030
the school did put on its plays.

01:30:06.030 --> 01:30:10.410
And I learned more from
being in those plays than I--

01:30:10.410 --> 01:30:12.870
probably in the end, it was
useful to me in later life

01:30:12.870 --> 01:30:13.740
than I did from--

01:30:16.760 --> 01:30:19.040
I'm sorry to say
this, because I wasn't

01:30:19.040 --> 01:30:21.230
very good at ancient
Greek, but possibly more

01:30:21.230 --> 01:30:23.880
than I did from
studying ancient Greek.

01:30:23.880 --> 01:30:29.570
But there is always a tendency,
and it's true in Australia too,

01:30:29.570 --> 01:30:31.730
and it's true in England,
as well as in America.

01:30:31.730 --> 01:30:33.230
Americans are not
sort of isolated--

01:30:33.230 --> 01:30:35.316
some bunch of 260
million isolated

01:30:35.316 --> 01:30:36.440
Philistines in this regard.

01:30:36.440 --> 01:30:41.900
They're all over the world, to
regard the sphere of the arts

01:30:41.900 --> 01:30:44.420
as being in some way
somewhat optional.

01:30:44.420 --> 01:30:45.600
It's the icing on the cake.

01:30:45.600 --> 01:30:49.670
You know, it's the meringue,
but the real cake is the sponge,

01:30:49.670 --> 01:30:52.730
and that's underneath.

01:30:52.730 --> 01:30:56.750
I believe in a much more
organically knit kind

01:30:56.750 --> 01:31:00.290
of conception of cultural
activity than that.

01:31:00.290 --> 01:31:08.410
But I'm sorry one
of the first things

01:31:08.410 --> 01:31:15.090
is to go in the small term
when cuts have to be made

01:31:15.090 --> 01:31:18.900
is the supposedly less
important art classes.

01:31:18.900 --> 01:31:21.630
But that is just something that,
you know, one is dealing with,

01:31:21.630 --> 01:31:25.620
and it's the same all over the
world in moments of shrinking

01:31:25.620 --> 01:31:28.560
educational possibility.

01:31:28.560 --> 01:31:29.370
You know?

01:31:29.370 --> 01:31:32.280
The visual literacy
of Americans,

01:31:32.280 --> 01:31:33.750
you know, many
Americans compared

01:31:33.750 --> 01:31:36.270
to that of the almost
complete visual indifference

01:31:36.270 --> 01:31:40.480
of many French that I
know, is staggering.

01:31:40.480 --> 01:31:44.474
But you know, it's always
going to seem somehow optional.

01:31:44.474 --> 01:31:46.140
And this was one of
the situations which

01:31:46.140 --> 01:31:48.120
the NEA was set up to solve.

01:31:48.120 --> 01:31:49.980
And on the whole, I
think with the usual,

01:31:49.980 --> 01:31:53.100
with all the admitted sort
of losses and imperfections,

01:31:53.100 --> 01:31:56.130
you know, and outright waste
of money, and confusions,

01:31:56.130 --> 01:32:01.850
and controversies, it has on the
whole worked out pretty well.

01:32:01.850 --> 01:32:02.820
There.

01:32:02.820 --> 01:32:03.790
AUDIENCE: Yes.

01:32:03.790 --> 01:32:07.670
My name is Ellen Parker, and I'm
the co-author of "The National

01:32:07.670 --> 01:32:10.095
Endowment for the Arts
Summary Report on the National

01:32:10.095 --> 01:32:13.975
Choreographers Study" coming
out later this summer.

01:32:13.975 --> 01:32:19.806
In hearing the report on
[INAUDIBLE] processes, in 1965,

01:32:19.806 --> 01:32:22.873
choreographers were
the first artist group

01:32:22.873 --> 01:32:24.610
funded by the Endowment.

01:32:24.610 --> 01:32:26.892
And they selected
seven choreographers,

01:32:26.892 --> 01:32:32.664
including the grand Alvin Ailey,
Merce Cunningham, José Limon.

01:32:32.664 --> 01:32:37.965
Now, anyone who considers
himself or herself to be

01:32:37.965 --> 01:32:41.882
a choreographer and has a
modicum of writing ability

01:32:41.882 --> 01:32:43.370
can apply to the Endowment.

01:32:43.370 --> 01:32:44.362
HUGHES: I'm sorry.

01:32:44.362 --> 01:32:45.354
Anybody who considers--

01:32:45.354 --> 01:32:48.330
AUDIENCE: --who has a
modicum of writing ability.

01:32:48.330 --> 01:32:50.330
One has to be able to write--

01:32:50.330 --> 01:32:50.926
HUGHES: Yes.

01:32:50.926 --> 01:32:52.634
AUDIENCE: --in order
to have one's talent

01:32:52.634 --> 01:32:54.107
considered by the Endowment.

01:32:54.107 --> 01:32:58.778
My question is, what do we
do about the pool of artists

01:32:58.778 --> 01:33:03.524
who are either so disaffected or
disinterested that they're not

01:33:03.524 --> 01:33:08.034
going to make applications to
any funding agency who do have

01:33:08.034 --> 01:33:10.379
talent, who are out there?

01:33:10.379 --> 01:33:13.860
How do we find today's
talent in America?

01:33:13.860 --> 01:33:20.560
Do we rely upon mere ability
to write the case of their art?

01:33:20.560 --> 01:33:21.940
HUGHES: Yes.

01:33:21.940 --> 01:33:23.570
Because if they
don't approach you,

01:33:23.570 --> 01:33:24.804
how can we know about them?

01:33:24.804 --> 01:33:25.748
AUDIENCE: Exactly.

01:33:25.748 --> 01:33:28.108
But if they can't write,
or don't write, or won't

01:33:28.108 --> 01:33:31.397
write, how do we find those?

01:33:31.397 --> 01:33:33.480
HUGHES: I don't know many
artists who can't write.

01:33:36.511 --> 01:33:39.010
I mean, I suppose
such may exist.

01:33:39.010 --> 01:33:42.790
The point is not that you find
them, but that they find you.

01:33:42.790 --> 01:33:44.010
I mean, the NEA is out there.

01:33:44.010 --> 01:33:45.790
Everybody knows it exists.

01:33:45.790 --> 01:33:49.510
And if the artist does not
wish to apply for an NEA grant,

01:33:49.510 --> 01:33:52.960
that is that artist's
absolute right,

01:33:52.960 --> 01:33:57.740
as well as it is his
or her right to apply.

01:33:57.740 --> 01:34:01.760
The NEA is not a
missionary institution.

01:34:01.760 --> 01:34:05.060
It's one which responds
to applications,

01:34:05.060 --> 01:34:06.010
as I understand it.

01:34:06.010 --> 01:34:07.910
But perhaps you feel
it should be a more

01:34:07.910 --> 01:34:09.790
missionary institution.

01:34:09.790 --> 01:34:13.590
AUDIENCE: I feel convinced
that there is talent in America

01:34:13.590 --> 01:34:16.378
which is not known, and--

01:34:16.378 --> 01:34:17.794
HUGHES: That is
true of all times.

01:34:17.794 --> 01:34:20.880
AUDIENCE: Artists that are more
busy struggling to survive,

01:34:20.880 --> 01:34:24.650
to create their art, whether
it's dance, or music,

01:34:24.650 --> 01:34:28.780
or theatre, not all of them
have the time nor the expertise.

01:34:28.780 --> 01:34:31.628
They're not born with
a god-given ability

01:34:31.628 --> 01:34:35.372
to write a grant application
and make a persuasive argument.

01:34:35.372 --> 01:34:36.830
HUGHES: Well, in
that case, perhaps

01:34:36.830 --> 01:34:38.330
you should streamline
and simplify

01:34:38.330 --> 01:34:41.596
the process of
grant application.

01:34:41.596 --> 01:34:45.910
[APPLAUSE]

01:34:45.910 --> 01:34:47.080
I'm sorry.

01:34:47.080 --> 01:34:48.598
I can suggest no
other alternative.

01:34:48.598 --> 01:34:50.056
AUDIENCE: I just
wanted to know how

01:34:50.056 --> 01:34:54.370
you would force the cream
to the top to suggest--

01:34:54.370 --> 01:34:56.680
HUGHES: You can't force
the cream to the top.

01:34:56.680 --> 01:35:02.118
The cream has to float of
its own molecular nature.

01:35:02.118 --> 01:35:03.574
[APPLAUSE]

01:35:03.574 --> 01:35:04.074
Yes?

01:35:07.497 --> 01:35:09.453
AUDIENCE: I'm Catherine Shu.

01:35:09.453 --> 01:35:15.340
I'm acting director of Chinese
Culture and Collection.

01:35:15.340 --> 01:35:17.840
The purpose of our
organization is

01:35:17.840 --> 01:35:23.328
to fulfill the needs of
American majority society needs,

01:35:23.328 --> 01:35:27.810
which we have a tremendous
need to understand

01:35:27.810 --> 01:35:31.794
the other side of the
world, and that we

01:35:31.794 --> 01:35:36.774
as a small group,
concerned citizens, most

01:35:36.774 --> 01:35:43.746
are immigrants from China,
Taiwan, or Hong Kong.

01:35:43.746 --> 01:35:48.726
We took an initiative to be
a self-support organization

01:35:48.726 --> 01:35:54.220
and to provide [INAUDIBLE]
workshops coinciding

01:35:54.220 --> 01:35:58.924
with school education,
mostly elementary school,

01:35:58.924 --> 01:36:01.786
middle school, and high school.

01:36:01.786 --> 01:36:07.750
My question is, as national
federal support organization,

01:36:07.750 --> 01:36:14.003
how do we support the initiative
adverse to an organization

01:36:14.003 --> 01:36:15.450
like us.

01:36:15.450 --> 01:36:20.016
So far, I, myself, and
several board members

01:36:20.016 --> 01:36:24.870
have called upon National
Endowment for Arts.

01:36:24.870 --> 01:36:27.111
HUGHES: With any success?

01:36:27.111 --> 01:36:30.789
AUDIENCE: I'm very sorry
to say that no success.

01:36:30.789 --> 01:36:35.700
They have suggest us to
provide coupons for scholars,

01:36:35.700 --> 01:36:40.327
for professors, for
their study groups.

01:36:40.327 --> 01:36:43.764
However, the federal
funding doesn't

01:36:43.764 --> 01:36:48.674
go to the common elementary,
high schools now.

01:36:48.674 --> 01:36:51.129
I'm very puzzled.

01:36:51.129 --> 01:36:52.538
HUGHES: You see,
unfortunately, I

01:36:52.538 --> 01:36:55.090
can't help you, because
I do not know anything--

01:36:55.090 --> 01:36:58.270
I'm a journalist, and I've never
applied for a grant in my life.

01:36:58.270 --> 01:37:02.870
And so I don't know what the
procedure is for application

01:37:02.870 --> 01:37:04.135
to, you know, foundations.

01:37:04.135 --> 01:37:06.010
AUDIENCE: They say they
don't have a category

01:37:06.010 --> 01:37:07.510
to fit into our needs.

01:37:07.510 --> 01:37:09.170
HUGHES: Well, in that case--

01:37:12.432 --> 01:37:14.890
wouldn't it possibly fall under
the purview of the National

01:37:14.890 --> 01:37:15.810
Endowment of the Humanities?

01:37:15.810 --> 01:37:16.560
I just don't know.

01:37:16.560 --> 01:37:17.750
I'm sorry.

01:37:17.750 --> 01:37:19.930
I can't [INAUDIBLE].

01:37:19.930 --> 01:37:21.128
I can't answer the question.

01:37:21.128 --> 01:37:24.161
AUDIENCE: I'm glad you say
you don't know because that's

01:37:24.161 --> 01:37:26.355
the reason I, myself,
and my colleagues

01:37:26.355 --> 01:37:27.355
come to this convention.

01:37:27.355 --> 01:37:30.501
Hopefully we have
better luck next time.

01:37:30.501 --> 01:37:32.000
HUGHES: I think
probably if you were

01:37:32.000 --> 01:37:35.600
to ask somebody who actually
works within the NEA

01:37:35.600 --> 01:37:37.340
and works within
funding organizations,

01:37:37.340 --> 01:37:39.650
you might very well get an
answer to that question.

01:37:39.650 --> 01:37:41.030
But I myself cannot supply that.

01:37:41.030 --> 01:37:42.580
HARRIS: Also, this
is the last one.

01:37:42.580 --> 01:37:43.079
HUGHES: OK.

01:37:43.079 --> 01:37:44.235
This is the last one.

01:37:44.235 --> 01:37:45.225
Sir?

01:37:45.225 --> 01:37:46.725
AUDIENCE: My name
is George Bennett,

01:37:46.725 --> 01:37:48.690
and I'm a student
in Boston here.

01:37:48.690 --> 01:37:50.175
I have a question.

01:37:50.175 --> 01:37:52.650
You pointed out that
the relative value

01:37:52.650 --> 01:37:55.674
of NEA funding in seven
days was higher than it

01:37:55.674 --> 01:37:57.507
has been in any time since?

01:37:57.507 --> 01:38:00.090
HUGHES: Well, the actual value
of watching it for your dollar.

01:38:00.090 --> 01:38:00.561
Yeah.

01:38:00.561 --> 01:38:01.186
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

01:38:01.186 --> 01:38:01.974
Exactly.

01:38:01.974 --> 01:38:03.765
It's my understanding
that during the '70s,

01:38:03.765 --> 01:38:08.404
the NEA funded relatively safe
forms of art and symphonies

01:38:08.404 --> 01:38:10.320
and dance companies.

01:38:10.320 --> 01:38:14.027
I'm wondering if you feel that
half the reason it exists now

01:38:14.027 --> 01:38:16.973
is necessarily non-political,
or whether it may possibly

01:38:16.973 --> 01:38:20.532
be an inevitable result of
the more challenging work

01:38:20.532 --> 01:38:23.747
that the NEA has funded
in the last few years?

01:38:23.747 --> 01:38:25.330
HUGHES: Well, I think
that, certainly,

01:38:25.330 --> 01:38:27.375
the NEA has come under
fire because some of the--

01:38:27.375 --> 01:38:29.000
I mean, obviously,
it's come under fire

01:38:29.000 --> 01:38:32.140
because some of the
material that it's funded

01:38:32.140 --> 01:38:34.270
has been found morally
offensive by others.

01:38:34.270 --> 01:38:35.560
That we take for granted.

01:38:35.560 --> 01:38:39.020
And I think that if you were to
look through the, as it were,

01:38:39.020 --> 01:38:41.200
the funding annals
of the NEA, you

01:38:41.200 --> 01:38:43.120
would find that only a
small number of grants,

01:38:43.120 --> 01:38:47.290
and most of those, in fact, in
the 1980s rather than the '70s,

01:38:47.290 --> 01:38:51.310
caused any kind of
kerfuffle at all.

01:38:51.310 --> 01:38:56.560
It does not-- you see, I have
to say that I'm rather opposed

01:38:56.560 --> 01:38:59.080
to this conventionalized
language of saying

01:38:59.080 --> 01:39:03.280
that things like symphony
concerts, and dance,

01:39:03.280 --> 01:39:05.510
and so on, and so
forth are safe culture.

01:39:05.510 --> 01:39:14.200
I mean, first of all, what
sort of value, I suppose,

01:39:14.200 --> 01:39:17.270
to attach relatively to
safe and unsafe culture.

01:39:17.270 --> 01:39:19.870
I mean, it seems to me
that the performance

01:39:19.870 --> 01:39:30.880
of say, a piece of music
by Hindemith, or Mozart,

01:39:30.880 --> 01:39:33.640
or whoever, the
results of it are

01:39:33.640 --> 01:39:36.580
not to be classified in terms
of either safety or unsafety,

01:39:36.580 --> 01:39:39.470
but rather in terms of the
sort of aesthetic pleasure

01:39:39.470 --> 01:39:42.100
and enlightenment that the
audience might get from them.

01:39:42.100 --> 01:39:48.100
I mean the idea that the NEA has
to be strung out between works

01:39:48.100 --> 01:39:49.142
of art which are--

01:39:49.142 --> 01:39:51.100
well, nobody uses the
word avant garde anymore,

01:39:51.100 --> 01:39:54.970
but cutting edge and
those which are not,

01:39:54.970 --> 01:39:57.137
and that it should be
supporting cutting edge stuff

01:39:57.137 --> 01:39:58.720
and shouldn't be
supporting the other,

01:39:58.720 --> 01:40:00.040
I think basically that's hokum.

01:40:02.920 --> 01:40:06.430
Because value does not
rise from anywhere else

01:40:06.430 --> 01:40:08.040
except deep inside
the work of art.

01:40:08.040 --> 01:40:12.887
It doesn't rise primarily
from its supposed power

01:40:12.887 --> 01:40:15.220
of social contestation,
although that may have something

01:40:15.220 --> 01:40:16.960
to do with it.

01:40:16.960 --> 01:40:20.020
It isn't the mandate of
the NEA to constantly fund

01:40:20.020 --> 01:40:25.270
works of which will
notionally at all points

01:40:25.270 --> 01:40:27.160
challenge the status quo.

01:40:27.160 --> 01:40:30.250
The status quo in America
is enormously tolerant.

01:40:30.250 --> 01:40:35.080
The status quo is, you know,
if you're talking about taste,

01:40:35.080 --> 01:40:37.430
includes just about
everything and its opposite.

01:40:37.430 --> 01:40:41.090
It is a big rubber wall,
which it is really hard

01:40:41.090 --> 01:40:45.640
to make a permanent dent
in, because America's

01:40:45.640 --> 01:40:48.310
institutional culture
for the last 70 years

01:40:48.310 --> 01:40:52.030
has been nothing
other than the new.

01:40:52.030 --> 01:40:56.080
Therefore, this is
why I keep saying

01:40:56.080 --> 01:41:04.000
that what the NEA ought to be
going for is art which is, sort

01:41:04.000 --> 01:41:06.490
of trying to encourage works
of art rather upon the guesses

01:41:06.490 --> 01:41:08.950
it can make about
their aesthetic quality

01:41:08.950 --> 01:41:11.620
than their capacity for
social contestation.

01:41:11.620 --> 01:41:13.590
Now you can have very
good works of art,

01:41:13.590 --> 01:41:19.280
you can have great works of art,
that are socially challenging.

01:41:19.280 --> 01:41:20.090
Of course you can.

01:41:20.090 --> 01:41:21.380
Always have had.

01:41:21.380 --> 01:41:28.294
But the idea that the NEA should
be looking out specifically

01:41:28.294 --> 01:41:30.710
for works of art which are,
for instance, political rather

01:41:30.710 --> 01:41:32.480
than nonpolitical, I
think, is something

01:41:32.480 --> 01:41:33.771
of a distortion of its mission.

01:41:36.651 --> 01:41:38.150
No, I'm not going
to get off on this

01:41:38.150 --> 01:41:40.310
because I've said it before.

01:41:40.310 --> 01:41:41.412
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:41:41.412 --> 01:41:42.370
HUGHES: Yeah, go ahead.

01:41:42.370 --> 01:41:42.870
Sorry.

01:41:42.870 --> 01:41:50.860
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:41:50.860 --> 01:41:55.669
HUGHES: Well, I think the
NEA was poleaxed by what

01:41:55.669 --> 01:41:56.710
went on in the late '80s.

01:41:56.710 --> 01:41:57.770
It wasn't expecting it.

01:41:57.770 --> 01:42:00.830
It didn't know how
to deal with it,

01:42:00.830 --> 01:42:04.520
and it proved remarkably
inept at responding to it.

01:42:04.520 --> 01:42:06.860
And of course, you
know, there was

01:42:06.860 --> 01:42:09.530
a sense of disorientation and
loss of direction and loss

01:42:09.530 --> 01:42:10.060
of mission.

01:42:10.060 --> 01:42:13.370
This, I think,
combined with the fact

01:42:13.370 --> 01:42:17.330
that the man who was running
it at the time, Frohnmeyer,

01:42:17.330 --> 01:42:20.030
was not the captain one would
wanted to have on the ship when

01:42:20.030 --> 01:42:22.580
the typhoon hit.

01:42:22.580 --> 01:42:25.304
Sure, I mean the NEA has got
a lot of ground to make up.

01:42:25.304 --> 01:42:26.720
I mean, the NEA
has got to go back

01:42:26.720 --> 01:42:29.176
to being a pretty
tough organization.

01:42:29.176 --> 01:42:31.128
Otherwise, it's
going to go down.

01:42:37.799 --> 01:42:39.424
AUDIENCE: My names
is [? Hal Tobich, ?]

01:42:39.424 --> 01:42:41.376
and I'm [INAUDIBLE].

01:42:41.376 --> 01:42:45.130
Would you be interested
in heading up the NEA?

01:42:45.130 --> 01:42:45.630
HUGHES: No.

01:42:48.950 --> 01:42:52.550
Here I am, late in middle
age, not a citizen.

01:42:54.774 --> 01:42:56.190
I mean, do you
think that I'd want

01:42:56.190 --> 01:42:58.370
to spend the rest of
my life butting horns

01:42:58.370 --> 01:43:02.760
with this bunch of
dinosaurs and madmen?

01:43:02.760 --> 01:43:04.652
AUDIENCE: Well, I reckon
it's sort of jail.

01:43:04.652 --> 01:43:06.100
HUGHES: Yeah, right.

01:43:06.100 --> 01:43:10.370
[LAUGHING]

01:43:10.370 --> 01:43:15.130
This is a job which nobody
in his right mind who

01:43:15.130 --> 01:43:17.390
wants to write or paint
or do anything himself

01:43:17.390 --> 01:43:18.990
would want to do that.

01:43:18.990 --> 01:43:20.914
AUDIENCE: We need
a tough cookie.

01:43:20.914 --> 01:43:22.357
HUGHES: You need a tough cookie.

01:43:22.357 --> 01:43:23.319
AUDIENCE: Do you
have anybody in mind?

01:43:23.319 --> 01:43:24.770
Can you think of anybody?

01:43:24.770 --> 01:43:28.834
HUGHES: I mean, my candidate for
the NEA would be John Brademas,

01:43:28.834 --> 01:43:29.625
but he can't do it.

01:43:29.625 --> 01:43:31.504
He doesn't want to do it.

01:43:31.504 --> 01:43:33.420
I mean, I would really
like to see-- you know,

01:43:33.420 --> 01:43:36.730
Brademas would be
superb in that job.

01:43:36.730 --> 01:43:41.760
But it's a very tough job,
and the rewards for it

01:43:41.760 --> 01:43:43.459
are not actually very high.

01:43:43.459 --> 01:43:45.250
You know, it's there
a problem-- you create

01:43:45.250 --> 01:43:48.730
one ingrate and 20 malcontents.

01:43:48.730 --> 01:43:51.205
OK, thank you very much.

01:43:51.205 --> 01:43:55.660
[APPLAUSE]

01:44:00.610 --> 01:44:06.055
[LAUGHING]

01:44:18.430 --> 01:44:20.680
HARRIS: Thank you
very much for coming.

01:44:20.680 --> 01:44:24.000
I daresay that not
all of our panelists

01:44:24.000 --> 01:44:25.410
would have agreed
with everything

01:44:25.410 --> 01:44:28.800
that was said tonight,
and I did resist

01:44:28.800 --> 01:44:34.470
the urge, a sort of professorial
urge, to call on them

01:44:34.470 --> 01:44:37.595
and say, Mr. Hansel, what
do you think of that?

01:44:37.595 --> 01:44:38.220
HUGHES: Manana.

01:44:38.220 --> 01:44:41.020
HARRIS: But you will hear
tomorrow if you come.

01:44:41.020 --> 01:44:43.110
I hope you will all be there.

01:44:43.110 --> 01:44:45.540
And it does begin at 9:15.

01:44:45.540 --> 01:44:46.830
I look forward to seeing you.

01:44:46.830 --> 01:44:49.910
Thank you, and thank
you, Robert Hughes.

